cal requirements** of a continuous path mapping from the unit interval $[0, 1]$.\n\n### 1. Why it is Connected (The Global View)\nTwo sets are disconnected if they can be separated by disjoint open regions. The graph $G$ (wiggles) and the segment $L$ (vertical bar) are "intertwined." Every neighborhood of a point on the segment $L$ contains points from the graph $G$. Conversely, any point on $G$ can be arbitrarily close to some point on $L$. You cannot cut a line separating the wiggles from the bar without slicing through the bar itself or the infinite stack of wiggles. Thus, topological connectivity holds.\n\n### 2. Why it is NOT Path-Connected (The Local View & The Picture)\nThe user asks: "Can\'t see the picture of why you can\'t draw a path."\nImagine drawing a string from a point on the wiggly graph to a point on the vertical bar.\n\n**The Obstacle: Density of Oscillations**\nAs you approach $x = 0$, the sine wave oscillates between $y = 1$ and $y = -1$ infinitely many times.\n*   **Constraint:** A continuous path $\\gamma: [0, 1] \\to X$ must preserve continuity. This means the limit of the position on the y-axis as you approach the bar must equal the position of the bar itself.\n*   **Impossibility:** Suppose you start at a point on the bar at height $y=0.5$. To reach a point on the graph $(x, \\sin(1/x))$ where $x$ is very small, your path must pass through the graph.\n*   **The Contradiction:** For the path to remain continuous at the transition from $x=0$ (the bar) to $x>0$ (the graph), the y-coordinate of your path on the graph side must converge to $0.5$ as $x \\to 0$. However, the equation $y = \\sin(1/x)$ does not converge to a single value as $x \\to 0$; it oscillates denser and denser.\n*   **Analogy:** Think of trying to park a car perfectly parallel to a wall that vibrates infinitely fast between $y=-1$ and $y=1$. You can vibrate the steering wheel, but to enter the parking spot (the segment) continuously, you must settle at a specific height. Once