{'conversation_id': 'conv_5869', 'example_id': 'conv_5869_message_2', 'category': 'earth_science', 'prompt_hash': 'bb241a624b2e0fa3ba27b08ff22cccf7717dd3f5fd4d027d1e3fe19f343afc7f', 'user_question': "I've always been told slabs sink into the mantle because they're cold and dense, but seismic tomography shows some slabs stagnating at the 660 km discontinuity while others punch through. What actually controls whether a slab stalls at the transition zone versus continuing into the lower mantle?", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a geophysicist who studies subduction zone dynamics and slab behavior in the mantle. Discuss with technical precision but grounded intuition.'}], 'reference_answer': 'The short version is that slab fate depends on a competition between the phase-transition buoyancy barrier at 660 km and the accumulated negative buoyancy of the slab — modulated heavily by trench kinematics and lower-mantle viscosity. No single parameter is decisive.\n\n**The barrier itself.** The ringwoodite breakdown to bridgmanite + ferropericlase has a negative Clapeyron slope. Recent multi-anvil work (Ishii et al. 2018, Chanyshev et al. 2022) has actually trimmed the magnitude from the classical ~-4 MPa/K down to roughly -2 MPa/K, which weakens the barrier. But for cold slabs (~800 K colder than ambient), that still translates to the transition happening ~30-50 km deeper inside the slab than outside, which is buoyant resistance on the order of 10^12 N/m of trench — non-trivial but not overwhelming.\n\n**Why some slabs stall.** The most robust correlation in numerical models (Čížková & Bina 2013, 2015; Goes et al. 2017) is with trench motion. When a trench retreats oceanward faster than the slab sinks (Izu-Bonin has retreated ~1000 km since the early Miocene), the slab is forced to lie flat against the 660. You get that characteristic horizontal "pancake" seen in tomography beneath eastern China extending ~2000 km inland. Stationary or advancing trenches (much of the western Pacific arc system during short windows) let the slab sink vertically, and it pierces.\n\n**Slab strength matters.** Old slabs (Pacific plate >120 Ma under Tonga) have cold, strong cores. Counterintuitively, stronger slabs are *more* likely to stagnate because they resist bending into the lower mantle and instead buckle and fold at the transition zone. Younger, weaker slabs (Cascadia-type) deform more easily and thread through.\n\n**The avalanche hypothesis.** Fukao & Obayashi (2013) argued stagnation is transient on 100-300 Myr timescales. Once enough mass accumulates, the pile becomes gravitationally unstable and drops as a slab avalanche — this may be what we see as remnant "blobs" deep in the lower mantle beneath North America (the Farallon remnants imaged by Sigloch, van der Meer) or under southern Africa. So the dichotomy may be a snapshot, not a permanent state.\n\n**Caveats.** Tomographic resolution at 660 km is ~200-400 km horizontally, which is comparable to slab width, so amplitude recovery is imperfect. P-wave models (GAP-P4, MIT-P) and S-wave models (SEMum2, SEMUCB-WM1) don\'t always agree on whether a slab is truly stagnant or just slowed. Also, the lower-mantle viscosity structure is debated — if there\'s a viscosity hill around 1000 km (as Rudolph et al. 2015 argue from geoid inversions), slabs may stall there rather than at 660, which is consistent with recent tomography showing a lot of slab material pooling around 1000-1200 km.\n\nSo the honest answer: thermally old + fast-retreating trench + strong Clapeyron barrier + high lower-mantle viscosity = stagnation, at least temporarily. Reverse those and you get penetration. But we\'re still arguing about the relative weights, and whether mid-mantle viscosity structure deserves more attention than the 660 transition it has historically gotten.', 'leakage_group': 'conv_5869', 'source_prompt_hash': '6e144ec98804469527bce56c4ea0edd272fed4cef3f94478fcd300bb996280dd', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 476, 'reference_tokens': 787, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not repeat the thinking boundary or reopen thinking\nin the final answer. Do not quote these instructions.\n\