{'conversation_id': 'conv_2992', 'example_id': 'conv_2992_message_2', 'category': 'medicine', 'prompt_hash': '35245cfc56a5c3827547b7787b3f33de2506a7f118d3fc4d43d38e860ac728b6', 'user_question': 'I had a patient on carbamazepine for epilepsy who got pregnant. Her OB put her on oral contraceptives postpartum for birth control. Nobody caught the interaction for months. Explain this so it never happens again.', 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a pharmacologist with clinical expertise. Teach the CYP450 system through cases, not abstractions.'}], 'reference_answer': 'This is one of the most clinically consequential drug interactions in medicine, and it gets missed because the neurologist and the OB each stay in their own lane.\n\n**The mechanism**\n\nCarbamazepine is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 — the enzyme responsible for metabolizing ethinyl estradiol and most progestins. "Induction" means carbamazepine activates the pregnane X receptor (PXR), which is a nuclear transcription factor that upregulates CYP3A4 gene expression. The liver produces more CYP3A4 enzyme protein, increasing the metabolic capacity for any CYP3A4 substrate. The effect takes 1-2 weeks to develop fully (time to synthesize new enzyme) and persists for 2-3 weeks after the inducer is stopped.\n\nThe result: ethinyl estradiol is metabolized so rapidly that plasma levels drop below the threshold (~35 pg/mL) needed to reliably suppress ovulation. The contraceptive failure rate on enzyme-inducing AEDs can exceed 3-6% per year — a three to sixfold increase over typical use failure rates.\n\n**The high-yield CYP3A4 inducer list**\n\nMemorize these — they all reduce hormonal contraceptive efficacy:\n- **Carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, oxcarbazepine** (AEDs)\n- **Rifampin** (the most potent inducer — can reduce OC efficacy by >50%)\n- **St. John\'s wort** (OTC supplement, patients don\'t report it)\n- **Some antiretrovirals**: efavirenz, nevirapine, ritonavir (at steady state)\n\nNotably, levetiracetam, lamotrigine, and valproate do NOT induce CYP3A4 and are safer with hormonal contraception (though lamotrigine has a bidirectional interaction — estrogen increases lamotrigine clearance, which can trigger seizures).\n\n**Safe contraceptive alternatives with enzyme inducers**\n\n- **Copper IUD**: no hormonal component, unaffected by enzyme induction. The best option.\n- **Levonorgestrel IUD (Mirena)**: the local progestin delivery is high enough that systemic metabolism doesn\'t significantly impair efficacy. Generally considered acceptable.\n- **Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (Depo-Provera)**: the high circulating progestin level provides a buffer, though some authorities recommend shorter injection intervals (10 weeks instead of 12).\n- **NOT reliable**: combined OCs, patch, ring, progestin-only pills, etonogestrel implant (Nexplanon — despite package insert claims, case reports of failure exist with strong inducers).', 'leakage_group': 'conv_2992', 'source_prompt_hash': '7739839cb250e9fcc5da5ecdf5b11c55199cbca3efd8c929d64f3acc6d8681dc', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 466, 'reference_tokens': 588, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential