l emphasize "Combined Oral Contraceptives".\n\n    Okay, ready.\n\n    (Self-Correction): Ensure I do not use bolding or headers in the final answer if that counts as markdown not allowed?\n    Instruction says: "give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation". Usually, bolding is okay in "plain prose". I will use paragraph breaks for clarity but minimal heavy formatting if possible, ensuring it reads like natural language.\n\n    Actually, looking at "final answer in plain prose", I should avoid bullet points if they look like lists. But paragraphs are fine. I will use standard paragraph structure.\n    Wait, I need to check "Check confounding, contrary evidence, applicability to the population, and unwarranted causal generalization."\n    Confounding: Patient factors (liver disease, genetics).\n    Contrary evidence: None really, strong consensus exists.\n    Uncertainty: Individual metabolic variability exists, but induction is strong enough to warrant avoidance.\n    Applicability: General rule applies to women of childbearing age on AEDs.\n\n    Okay, proceeding to generate the final response.\n</think>\n\nThe scenario described highlights a known and significant pharmacokinetic interaction between carbamazepine and oral contraceptives. To prevent recurrence, it is necessary to understand the biological mechanism, recognize the systemic vulnerabilities in healthcare workflows, and implement specific clinical safeguards.\n\nThe root cause is hepatic enzyme induction. Carbamazepine is an antiepileptic drug that potently induces the cytochrome P450 enzyme system, specifically CYP3A4, in the liver. These enzymes are responsible for metabolizing many substances in the body. Oral contraceptives containing estrogen and/or progestin are substrates of these same enzymes. When a patient takes carbamazepine alongside oral contraceptives, the drug accelerates the breakdown of the contraceptive hormones into inactive byproducts before they can re