{'conversation_id': 'conv_5847', 'example_id': 'conv_5847_message_2', 'category': 'medicine', 'prompt_hash': 'b706bc80d8148139dfe5b1b1ddea5f144b352035cc905a7c9cdb0feea8b1e466', 'user_question': "Migraine is a 'vascular headache' — but vasoconstriction can stop a migraine, and so can vasodilation. So which is it?", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a headache specialist who trained in the era when migraine was taught as a vascular disease and watched the field overturn that model. Be honest about what the old theory got right, where it failed, and what replaced it.'}], 'reference_answer': "The contradiction is real, and the resolution is that the vascular theory was wrong. Migraine is a brain disorder. Vessel diameter changes are downstream noise, not the signal.\n\n**The historical story.** Wolff in the 1940s watched extracranial arteries pulse during attacks, gave ergotamine (a vasoconstrictor), saw the headache improve, and concluded the pain came from dilated vessels. For 50 years that was the textbook. Triptans, developed in the 80s, fit the story: they're 5HT1B agonists, they constrict, they abort migraine. Done.\n\nThen the story fell apart.\n\n**Where it broke.** First, imaging. When MR angiography studies (Schoonman 2008, Amin 2013) actually measured vessel caliber during spontaneous and provoked migraine, the dilation was minimal or absent on the headache side, and pain didn't track with diameter. Second, nitroglycerin — a pure vasodilator — reliably triggers migraine in migraineurs hours after the vasodilation has resolved. The headache outlives the vessel changes. Third, and decisively: CGRP. Calcitonin gene-related peptide is a potent vasodilator, IV CGRP triggers migraine, but CGRP antagonists (gepants like ubrogepant, rimegepant; mAbs like erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab) abort and prevent migraine without producing meaningful vasoconstriction. Erenumab targets the CGRP receptor; the others target the peptide. None of them squeeze vessels. They all work.\n\nIf vasoconstriction were the mechanism, those drugs shouldn't work. They do. So vasoconstriction isn't the mechanism.\n\n**What replaced it: the trigeminovascular model.** The trigeminal nerve sends nociceptive afferents to the dura and large cerebral vessels. Activation of these afferents — driven centrally, not by vessel changes — releases CGRP, substance P, PACAP, and neurokinin A from the peripheral terminals. This produces neurogenic inflammation around dural vessels and, more importantly, antidromic pain signaling back through the trigeminal ganglion to the trigeminocervical complex in the brainstem and on to thalamus and cortex. The vessels do dilate a bit because CGRP is a vasodilator, but that's a marker of the process, not the cause of pain.\n\nWhat triggers the trigeminal activation? In migraine with aura, cortical spreading depression — Leão's slow propagating wave of depolarization followed by suppression — appears to activate trigeminal afferents at the meningeal interface. In migraine without aura, the trigger is presumably similar but subclinical or in regions that don't generate aura phenomenology. The hypothalamus lights up on PET in the prodrome, hours before pain, which is why people get the food cravings, yawning, and mood shifts before the headache.\n\n**So how do triptans actually work?** Mostly by 5HT1B/1D receptor activation on the trigeminal nerve terminals themselves, inhibiting CGRP release. The vasoconstriction is a side effect — and the reason triptans are contraindicated in coronary disease, recent stroke, or uncontrolled hypertension. Sumatriptan 6 mg SC, 50-100 mg PO; rizatriptan 10 mg ODT works fast; eletriptan 40-80 mg has the best efficacy data; frovatriptan has the longest half-life and is useful for menstrual migraine prophylaxis.\n\n**The clean proof.** Lasmiditan is a 5HT1F agonist — same family, no vascular activity at all. It aborts migraine. That experiment alone retires the vascular hypothesis. CGRP-targeting drugs do the same.\n\n**Practical upshot.** When you abort with a triptan or a gepant or relieve a chronic migraineur with erenumab, you're modulating a trigeminal pain pathway, not fixing a plumbing problem. Vasodilators trigger and vasoconstrictors abort because both interventions perturb a system the trigeminovascular circuit is sensitive to — but the circuit, not the vessel, is the disease.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_5847', 'source_prompt_hash': '972f102acc7768db4a0c3afd018c677d0c0295230b37d533314725e60a4fc4a6', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 451, 'reference_tokens': 908, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture