{'conversation_id': 'conv_6973', 'example_id': 'conv_6973_message_2', 'category': 'medicine', 'prompt_hash': '7a888d082cdd3d75be954be17dd3fb0f48c86098611f82e7e83151bd3476a8f6', 'user_question': "Walk me through the modern T2DM treatment ladder — metformin, then SGLT2i or GLP-1RA based on comorbidities, when and how insulin enters. Why guidelines moved away from the old 'metformin → sulfonylurea' default.", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an endocrinologist consulting with primary care colleagues. Explain treatment frameworks with the underlying physiology and trial evidence that justifies current practice.'}], 'reference_answer': "The modern ladder is fundamentally a comorbidity-stratified algorithm, not a sequential A1c-driven one. That's the biggest conceptual change.\n\n**Step 1: Metformin + lifestyle, still.** Metformin remains first-line for most patients — A1c reduction ~1-1.5%, weight neutral to mildly favorable, decades of safety data, cheap. The contraindication everyone misremembers: eGFR <30 (not <60). Between 30 and 45 you can continue at reduced dose with monitoring. Hold for contrast, acute illness, hypoxia.\n\n**Step 2: Branch on comorbidity, not A1c.** This is where the algorithm broke from the old default.\n\n- **Established ASCVD or high CV risk:** GLP-1RA with proven benefit (semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide) OR SGLT2i (empagliflozin, canagliflozin). LEADER showed liraglutide cut MACE 13%. SELECT showed semaglutide cut MACE 20% in non-diabetic obese patients with CVD — that's a profound finding.\n- **Heart failure (especially HFrEF, but also HFpEF post-EMPEROR-Preserved):** SGLT2i first. Empagliflozin cut HF hospitalization 35% in EMPA-REG, and the benefit appears in non-diabetic HF too (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced).\n- **CKD with albuminuria:** SGLT2i first (DAPA-CKD, EMPA-KIDNEY, FLOW with semaglutide). Slows GFR decline ~50%, reduces progression to ESRD.\n- **Obesity-dominant:** GLP-1RA, especially semaglutide or tirzepatide. Tirzepatide (dual GIP/GLP-1) is hitting 20%+ weight loss in SURMOUNT trials.\n- **No comorbidity, cost-sensitive:** Sulfonylurea or pioglitazone are still defensible.\n\nNotice these are added regardless of whether the patient is at A1c goal — the mortality and renal benefits are independent of glucose lowering.\n\n**Step 3: Combine.** SGLT2i + GLP-1RA is increasingly common — different mechanisms, additive A1c reduction, complementary CV/renal benefit. Cost is the limiting factor.\n\n**Step 4: Why sulfonylureas got demoted.** Three reasons. First, hypoglycemia — meaningful rates in elderly, with falls and ED visits. Second, weight gain (2-3 kg). Third, no CV benefit and probable beta-cell burnout accelerating insulin dependence. CAROLINA didn't show harm vs linagliptin but didn't show benefit either, and we now have agents that actively reduce mortality. Glipizide is still fine for a patient who can't afford newer agents — just not the default.\n\n**Step 5: Insulin entry.** Triggers: A1c >10% at diagnosis, symptomatic hyperglycemia (polyuria, weight loss), random glucose >300, or failure of triple therapy. Start basal — glargine or degludec, 10 units nightly or 0.1-0.2 u/kg — and titrate by fasting glucose, increasing 2 units every 3 days until FBG 80-130. Don't add prandial insulin until basal is optimized; many patients don't need it if they're also on a GLP-1RA, which blunts postprandial excursions. If you do add prandial, start with the largest meal and expand.\n\nThe philosophical shift: we stopped treating T2DM as a glucose disease and started treating it as a cardiometabolic disease where glucose is one of several outcomes. That's why the ladder reordered.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_6973', 'source_prompt_hash': '69625c293ae733f255d5154dc389e9c864007640ed21c04169d808cff008be60', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 480, 'reference_tokens': 847, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 