food chains hardest.\n\n**The Deccan Traps are real and relevant**\n\nThe Deccan large igneous province in western India erupted an estimated 1.3-1.5 million km³ of basalt between roughly 67 and 64 Ma. This is an extraordinary volume — enough to bury an area the size of France under 2 km of lava. The eruptions released massive amounts of CO2 and SO2, and mercury anomalies in K-Pg boundary sections worldwide correlate with volcanic pulses.\n\nSome marine groups — particularly certain foraminifera and ammonite lineages — show diversity declines beginning 100,000-300,000 years before the impact, which your colleague would argue reflects Deccan-driven environmental stress.\n\n**The emerging synthesis**\n\nHull et al. (2020) in Science analyzed ocean temperature records across the K-Pg and found that Deccan-related warming occurred in the ~300,000 years leading up to the boundary, but the ecosystems had largely adjusted to it. The mass extinction signal itself is geologically instantaneous and coincides precisely with the impact. Their conclusion: the Deccan made things harder, but the impact was the lethal blow.\n\nAn additional twist: high-precision geochronology (Renne et al. 2015; Sprain et al. 2019) suggests the most voluminous phase of Deccan eruption occurred immediately AFTER the impact, possibly triggered by the massive seismic energy (~magnitude 11+ equivalent) transmitted through the Earth. If so, the post-impact Deccan activity may have impeded recovery rather than contributing to the initial kill.', 'leakage_group': 'conv_5117', 'source_prompt_hash': 'c5bd9ccb6cd39619c2d3ef5dbb772ebb92133e46b9c587387a703621a4536434', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 462, 'reference_tokens': 664, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user