{'conversation_id': 'conv_4095', 'example_id': 'conv_4095_message_2', 'category': 'science', 'prompt_hash': '91c30192ee1d2f81bc695803f03d20683c14d558dd36bb4bb582e6b479e74f29', 'user_question': 'Why does cooking food make it easier to digest? What chemistry, biology, and physics are involved?', 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a science and technology educator. Make complex topics accessible.'}], 'reference_answer': "Cooking is a profoundly transformative process that improves digestibility through interconnected physical, chemical, and biological mechanisms. Its importance is so fundamental that some anthropologists argue it was a key driver of human brain evolution.\n\nThe physics is straightforward: heat energy is transferred into food via conduction (a hot pan touching meat), convection (boiling water or hot air circulating in an oven), or radiation (infrared from glowing coals or a broiler element). This energy raises the internal temperature of the food, and temperature is the trigger for every chemical transformation that follows.\n\nThe most important chemical change is protein denaturation. Proteins in raw food are folded into compact three-dimensional shapes stabilized by hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic interactions, van der Waals forces, and disulfide bridges. Heating above roughly 40 to 70 degrees Celsius disrupts these interactions, causing the protein to unfold. This matters enormously for digestion because our proteolytic enzymes — pepsin in the stomach, trypsin and chymotrypsin in the small intestine — work by cleaving exposed peptide bonds along the protein backbone. A tightly folded globular protein hides most of its backbone internally, making enzymatic access difficult. An unfolded, denatured protein is an open chain, presenting its peptide bonds like a buffet line. Cooked egg protein is approximately 90 percent digestible compared to about 50 percent for raw egg — a near doubling of nutritional yield from the same food.\n\nStarch undergoes an equally dramatic transformation called gelatinization. Raw starch is packed into semi-crystalline granules composed of amylose and amylopectin polymers in a tightly ordered arrangement. When heated in the presence of water above 60 to 80 degrees Celsius, these granules absorb water, swell irreversibly, and lose their crystalline structure. The disordered gelatinized starch is far more accessible to salivary and pancreatic amylase. Raw potato starch is roughly 50 percent digestible; after cooking, digestibility rises to about 95 percent. For early humans dependent on tubers and wild grains, this difference was the margin between caloric adequacy and starvation.\n\nAt the cellular level, heat breaks down plant cell walls. Humans lack cellulase, the enzyme needed to digest cellulose, so the nutrients locked inside intact plant cells are partially inaccessible when eaten raw. Cooking softens and ruptures these walls, releasing their contents. This is why lycopene bioavailability from tomatoes increases two to threefold with cooking, and beta-carotene absorption from carrots increases roughly fivefold.\n\nCooking also destroys pathogens. Most foodborne bacteria — Salmonella, E. coli O157:H7, Campylobacter — are killed at sustained temperatures above 70 degrees Celsius. Parasitic cysts and larvae are similarly neutralized. The reduced disease burden from cooked food would have represented a significant survival advantage for early human populations.\n\nRichard Wrangham's cooking hypothesis ties all of these threads together evolutionary. By dramatically increasing the caloric yield of available foods, cooking may have provided the energy surplus needed to support the metabolically expensive human brain, which consumes roughly 20 percent of resting metabolic energy despite representing only 2 percent of body mass. Archaeological evidence of controlled fire use dates to roughly one to one-and-a-half million years ago, coinciding with the period of rapid encephalization in the Homo lineage — along with the reduction in jaw size, tooth size, and gut volume that would be expected if food were being externally processed by heat before consumption.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_4095', 'source_prompt_hash': '8ee385fa9d1e4945e6b0354e2d44db77d7bc972c88f251a8d51a565b60a5c74c', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 431, 'reference_tokens': 738, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one