{'conversation_id': 'conv_4963', 'example_id': 'conv_4963_message_2', 'category': 'medicine', 'prompt_hash': 'a24677a2b0bed6fc8001c041c7efe8a43ab2441264f549a5c7fc7cf3e9273d05', 'user_question': 'Walk me through the differential for chest pain — typical anginal vs atypical, ACS vs PE vs aortic dissection vs pericarditis vs musculoskeletal — and the bedside features that discriminate.', 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an attending cardiologist running a teaching session for senior internal medicine residents. Be specific about clinical features, mechanisms, and time-to-action.'}], 'reference_answer': "Start with the framing that matters: chest pain triage is a probabilistic exercise where you're trying to beat down four lethal diagnoses (ACS, PE, aortic dissection, tamponade) before settling on benign etiologies.\n\n**Typical anginal pain** has three classic features: substernal location, provoked by exertion or stress, relieved by rest or nitroglycerin. All three = typical (high pretest probability for CAD, ~90% in older men). Two = atypical. One or zero = non-anginal. The trap: women, diabetics, and older patients often present atypically — dyspnea on exertion, epigastric discomfort, or jaw/arm pain alone.\n\n**ACS discriminators:** pressure or heaviness rather than sharp; radiation to right arm or both arms (LR ~2.6 and ~7 respectively); diaphoresis; nausea; symptom duration >20 minutes at rest. Reproducibility on palpation drops likelihood but does NOT rule out — about 5–7% of MIs have reproducible chest wall tenderness. Use HEART score for risk stratification; troponin trajectory (0/1h or 0/3h hs-cTn algorithms) is the workhorse.\n\n**PE:** pleuritic pain, dyspnea out of proportion to exam, tachycardia, unilateral leg swelling, recent immobilization or surgery, hemoptysis. Apply PERC if low pretest; Wells or revised Geneva if intermediate. Hypoxia and a clear chest x-ray with tachycardia is PE until proven otherwise. S1Q3T3 is famous but insensitive (~20%).\n\n**Aortic dissection:** abrupt onset (peak intensity at onset, not crescendo), tearing or ripping quality, radiation to back or interscapular region, blood pressure differential >20 mmHg between arms, pulse deficit, new aortic insufficiency murmur, neuro deficits from arch vessel involvement. The ADD-RS score (high-risk conditions, pain features, exam findings) plus a D-dimer <500 ng/mL has good rule-out properties. CT angio is definitive. Big trap: type A dissection occluding the RCA ostium presents as inferior STEMI — giving lytics kills the patient. Always check for the dissection pattern before tPA in inferior MI: pulse deficit, BP differential, mediastinal widening on CXR, severe back pain.\n\n**Pericarditis:** sharp, pleuritic, positional (worse supine, better leaning forward), preceded by viral prodrome, friction rub (pathognomonic but transient and missed in ~70%). ECG: diffuse concave ST elevation with PR depression, reciprocal changes only in aVR/V1. Troponin elevation suggests myopericarditis. Watch for tamponade: pulsus paradoxus >10 mmHg, JVD with clear lungs, muffled heart sounds.\n\n**Musculoskeletal/costochondritis:** reproducibility on palpation, sharp and well-localized, worse with specific movements, no autonomic features, normal ECG and troponin. This is a diagnosis of exclusion in anyone with cardiac risk factors.\n\n**GI mimics worth mentioning:** esophageal spasm responds to nitroglycerin (so does angina — don't be fooled by relief). Boerhaave with mediastinitis can mimic dissection.\n\nBedside heuristic: if the pain is abrupt and maximal at onset, think dissection or PE. If it's crescendo with exertion, think ACS. If it's pleuritic and positional, think pericarditis or PE. If it's reproducible on palpation in a low-risk patient with normal ECG and troponin, MSK is reasonable — but never on the first encounter in a 65-year-old diabetic.\n\nWorkup minimum for any concerning chest pain: 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes, hs-troponin (serial), CXR, basic labs. Add CT angio with dissection protocol if any high-risk dissection feature.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_4963', 'source_prompt_hash': 'b0262fb89292ed603f0945393752c451910b3bacade1520ae8375a6855d3ca41', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 461, 'reference_tokens': 855, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not repeat the thinking boundary or reopen thinking\nin the final answer. Do not quote these instructions.\n\nSubject: medicine\nSubject-specific reasoning guidance:\nIdentify the relevant physiology, population, clinical context, and strength of available evidence.\nExplain mechanisms or interpret evidence with justified qualifications and appropriate uncertainty.\nCheck confounding, contrary evidence, applicability to the population, and unwarranted causal generalization."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Walk me through the differential for chest pain — typical anginal vs atypical, ACS vs PE vs aortic dissection vs pericarditis vs musculoskeletal — and the bedside features that discriminate.'}], 'completion': [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Thinking Process:\n\n1.  **Analyze the Request:**\n    *   **Topic:** Differential diagnosis of chest pain (medicine).\n    *   **Specific Comparisons:** Typical angina, Atypical angina, Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS), Pulmonary Embolism (PE), Aortic Dissection, Pericarditis, Musculoskeletal (Musculoskeletal/Costochondritis).\n    *   **Task:** Walk through the differential and explain bedside features that discriminate between them.\n    *   **Constraints:** Scientific accuracy, clear explanation, appropriate depth, identify false premises (none apparent in prompt initially), distinguish facts vs assumptions, avoid inventing sources/mechanisms, concise but thorough reasoning trace in native thinking, end with exactly one `\n\n</think> boundary, then a self-contained final answer.\n*   **Constraint Checklist & Confidence Score:**\n    1.  Answer scientific questions accurately? Yes.\n    2.  Clearly? Yes.\n    3.  Depth requested? Yes.\n    4.  Use user\'s actual question/context? Yes.\n    5.  Identify/correct false premises? (Check for misconceptions in common knowledge).\n    6.  Distinguish established facts, assumptions, conditional conclusions, uncertainty? Yes.\n    7.  Do not invent sources/measurements/experience/mechanisms? Yes.\n    8.  Prefer concise correct account? Yes.\n    9.  Consider valid alternative explanations? Yes.\n    10. Native thinking framework applied? Yes.\n    11. Check result/reasoning? Yes.\n    12. Finish thinking with exactly one native `\n</think>` boundary? Yes.\n    13. Self-contained final answer? Yes.\n    14. No repeat of thinking boundary in final answer? Yes.\n    15. No quote instructions? Yes.\n\n2.  **Native Thinking Framework:**\n    *   **Framework:** Clinical Reasoning / Differential Diagnosis based on Pathophysiology & Symptomatology.\n    *   **Key Dimensions:** Trigger/Pain Modifiers, Radiation, Associated Symptoms, Physical Exam Findings.\n    *   **Evidence Strength:** Strong consensus guidelines (ESC/AHA) for ACS/MI; clinical judgment/empiric for non-ischemic causes.\n    *   **Checks:**\n        *   Are "Atypical Angina" and "Typical Angina" defined correctly by ESC criteria (Stable Unstable)? Usually "Angina Pectoris" is classified as Stable vs. Unstable. "Atypical" usually refers to variant presentations of stable CAD not fitting typical exertion/radiation rules, or non-cardiac chest pain often mislabeled atypical. I need to clarify the terminology (Prague/Stability vs. Chest Pain Classification Systems like the Chicago classification). Actually, standard medical teaching often uses Typical, Atypical, Non-Coronary equivalent terms from the Canadian Chest Pain Study Group or similar. But clinically, "Atypical Angina" is often synonymous with Chronic Coronary Disease (CCD) pain without classic features, whereas "Acute Coronary Syndrome" implies ischemia of > hours duration or STEMI/NSTEMI. I must be precise here.\n        *   PE: Risk stratification (Wells score concept) but bedside is mostly tachycardia/inspiration dyspnea.\n        *   Dissection: Sudden onset, tearing, pulse deficit.\n        *   Pericarditis: Pleuritic, positional, friction rub.\n        *   Musculoskeletal: Reproducible tenderness.\n\n3.  **Drafting the Content - Step-by-Step:**\n    *   **Introduction:** Briefly define chest pain severity (emergent vs elective) and initial stabilization context (ABC).\n    *   **Angina Spectrum:**\n        *   *Typical:* Sublingual/breathless exertion -> relief with rest/nitrates. Radiates to jaw/arm.\n        *   *Atypical:* Less predictable pattern, no radiation, less associated with exertion.\n        *   *ACS:* Progressive/Unstable. Rest pain, crescendo frequency. *Crucial Distinction:* ACS = Ischemia causing ECG changes/Biomarkers (Time-sensitive). "Atypical Angina" is a historical term for chronic stable pain, whereas ACS is an acute event. Need to make sure I don\'t confuse "atypical" with "non-critical".\n    *   **PE:** Dyspnea dominant? Syncope? Hemoptysis? Tachycardia. *Bedside:* Hypoxia, JVD, absent/soft breath sounds, pleuritic pain. CTAPC.\n    *   **Dissection:** "Tearing/Ripping" quality. Intermittent BP pulse deficits. Neck bruits. Carotid/jaw pain.\n    *   **Pericarditis:** Sharp, pleuritic. Worse lying down/sitting up forward. Friction rub.\n    *   **Musculoskeletal:** Reproducible tenderness. Movement pain. No systemic signs.\n    *   **Synthesis/Discrimination:** A table or list of discriminating features (Quality, Location, Radiation, Timing, Aggravating/Relieving factors, Associated symptoms, Physical exam).\n    *   **Caveats:** Overlap exists (e.g., pericardial pain during MI, aortic dissection mimicking other things). Safety net: Rule out lethal causes first (ACS/Dissection).\n\n4.  **Refining the Content (Fact Checking & Nuance):**\n    *   *Terminology Correction:* In many guidelines (ACC/AHA/ESC), "Atypical Angina" is not a distinct pathological entity but rather a clinical descriptor for stable angina that doesn\'t fit Type II angina criteria, often implying NLA-angina (No Left Ventricle/Ischemia specific markers). Wait, the most accepted classification is:\n        1.  Typical Angina\n        2.  Atypical Angina (sometimes called Variant or Mild-Moderate)\n        3.  Non-Anginal (Non-cardiac)\n        However, modern practice emphasizes "Stable Angina" vs "Unstable Angina/ACS". I should use the terms provided by the user but ground them in physiology. I will treat "Atypical Angina" as chronic stable pain that does not meet full criteria for typical angina.\n    *   *ACS Definition:* It encompasses STEMI, NSTEMI, and Unstable Angina. The key discriminator is the presence of myocardial necrosis (Troponin rise) or dynamic ECG changes compared to baseline.\n    *   *Dissection:* Differentiate Type A (Thoracic) vs Type B (Descending). Bedside focus is pulse pressure difference.\n    *   *PE:* Don\'t rule out PE solely on lack of hypoxemia (normal oxygen can exist).\n    *   *Pericarditis:* ST elevation in widespread leads vs local (STEMI). ST depression + PR depression.\n    *   *Uncertainty:* Mention that physical exam findings overlap significantly, especially in early presentations or comorbidities.\n\n5.  **Reasoning Trace Check:**\n    *   Did I conflate terms? (Atypical angina vs Non-cardiac chest pain). Clarify that "Atypical" usually means "Symptoms suggest CAD but aren\'t Classic".\n    *   Did I prioritize safety? Yes, ACS and Dissection are top priorities.\n    *   Did I include mechanisms? Ischemia (supply/demand mismatch), Embolism (obstruction), Stretch/Tear (dissection), Inflammation (pericarditis).\n    *   Did I check confounding? Elderly patients may have silent ischemia or altered presentation.\n\n6.  **Final Polish:** Ensure the final answer stands alone. Keep it structured. Avoid unnecessary fluff. Focus on *bedside* discrimination.\n\n7.  **Output Generation Plan:**\n    *   Start with high-priority life threats (ACS/Dissection/PE).\n    *   Group by category logic (Cardiac, Pulmonary, Vascular, Others).\n    *   Detail the distinguishing features (History, Physical).\n    *   Add a concluding note on the limitation of physical exam.\n    *   End with the required delimiter.\n\n*(Self-Correction during drafting)*: Wait, the prompt asks specifically to walk through the differential for "typical anginal vs atypical, ACS vs PE...". "Typical Angina" is technically a subset of Stable Angina. "ACS" includes Unstable Angina (pre-necrosis) or active infarction. I need to be careful not to imply they are mutually exclusive categories where ACS includes all angina. ACS is defined by pathology (ischemia/ischemia + tissue injury). Atypical angina is usually a diagnostic label for pain characteristics suggesting CAD that don\'t fully fit the definition of typical angina (exertional, radiation).\nActually, under the ACC/AHA guidelines:\n1. Stable Angina Pectoris\n2. Unstable Angina (part of ACS spectrum, but non-infarction)\n3. NSTEMI/STEMI\nHowever, the "Canadian Chest Pain Questionnaire" classifies as "Classic (Typical)", "Moderate (Atypical)", "Low/Mild", "Non".\nBetter approach: Use the standard clinical triage categories while respecting the user\'s terminology.\nACS: Acute threat to myocardium (NSTEMI/STEMI/UA).\nStable Angina: Exertional pain relieved by rest/NTG.\n"Atypical": Pain suggestive of CAD but variable triggers/radiation or less predictable.\n*Decision:* I will clarify the terminology in the answer because "Atypical Angina" is historically used but "Stable" vs "Unstable" (ACS) is more clinically actionable. I will address the user\'s terms directly but contextualize them within current physiology.\n\n*Wait, let\'s double check definitions.*\nESC Guidelines: "Typical", "Atypical", "Non-variant".\nTypical: Pressure, squeezing, heaviness, substernal, >10 mins, reproducible trigger, relieved by NTG.\nAtypical: Any of these, but <100% reproducibility OR minor pain OR different radiation OR triggered by mild activity.\nACS: Suspected unstable plaque rupture/thrombus.\nOkay, I\'ll stick to the core differentiating features for each.\n\n*Physical Exam Checks:*\n*   Pericarditis: Costal tenderness? No. Pericardial friction rub? Yes. Stethoscope placement? Apical?\n*   Dissection: Carotid pulses? Femoral pulses? Blood pressure gradient?\n*   PE: Breath sounds (wheezes?), JVP? Tachypnea?\n\n*Conflicting Evidence:* Pericarditis *can* mimic MI (Elevated troponin in perimyocarditis). MI *can* cause pleuritic pain if there\'s effusion/pericardial involvement. This needs qualification.\n\n*Plan finalized:*\n1. High-yield summary table/list.\n2. Deep dive into specific discriminators.\n3. Emphasis on urgency.\n\n(End of thought process)\n</think>\n\nTo navigate this differential, we must anchor the discussion on the pathophysiology of the pain generation and the specific physiological stressors that precipitate or aggravate each condition. A critical starting assumption is that **chest pain is a symptom, not a diagnosis**, and the priority is distinguishing potentially fatal etiologies (ACS, Dissection, Massive PE) from benign ones using clinical patterns before laboratory confirmation. Below is a breakdown of the distinctions based on history, modality, and physical examination.\n\n### 1. Angina Spectrum: Typical vs. Atypical vs. ACS\n\nWhile all three share cardiac ischemia, their urgency and mechanism differ significantly regarding timing and stability.\n\n| Feature | Typical Angina (Stable) | Atypical Angina (Variant/Mild) | Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Onset** | Predictable. Usually occurs after exertion or emotional stress. | Variable. May occur with minimal exertion or at rest (but usually short-lived). | Sudden or progressively worsening. May occur at rest. |\n| **Duration** | Short (2-10 minutes). Resolves spontaneously with rest. | Similar to typical, but pattern is inconsistent ("unpredictable"). | Lasts > 10-15 minutes (if treated) or progresses to persistent pain. |\n| **Character** | Pressure, squeezing, heaviness, tightness. | Variable intensity. May feel sharp or burning. | Crushing, severe, unrelenting. |\n| **Radiation** | Classically left arm, jaw, neck, back. | Less common radiation, often truncal. | Often more diffuse radiation, though can remain localized. |\n| **Relief** | Immediate resolution with rest or sublingual nitrates. | Partial resolution with rest/NSAIDs. | Requires intervention (catheterization, meds); nitrates provide little relief. |\n| **Physiologic Mechanism** | Fixed coronary stenosis (plaque obstruction). Increased demand exceeds supply via collateral flow limit. | Variable coronary stenosis or hemodynamic compromise. | Plaque disruption $\\rightarrow$ thrombus formation $\\rightarrow$ luminal obstruction/embolus. |\n\n*Clinical Discrimination:* The primary distinction between **Typical** and **Atypical** is the **reproducibility** of the trigger and the response to nitroglycerin. Patients with typical angina have consistent symptoms. Atypical angina involves symptoms that do not fit this model, making them higher risk for future ACS events even if current pain is mild. However, **ACS** is distinguished by instability—either new onset chest pain, worsening of previous angina, or symptoms occurring at rest that persist. This requires immediate workup (ECG, Troponins).\n\n### 2. Pericarditis vs. Heart Failure vs. Ischemia (The Mimics)\n\nWhile Pericarditis is primarily inflammatory, its differentiation from ACS is crucial given overlapping ECG findings.\n\n| Feature | Pericarditis | Myocardial Infarction (STEMI/NSTEMI) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pain Quality** | Sharp, stabbing, pleuritic (associated with respiration). | Pressure, constriction, dull ache. |\n| **Positional Changes** | **Key feature:** Worsens lying supine; improves leaning forward. | Position often irrelevant unless severe HF present. |\n| **Associated Symptoms** | Dry cough, dyspnea, fever (malignancy/viral prodrome). | Diaphoresis, nausea, palpitations (autonomic surge). |\n| **Exam Findings** | **Pericardial Friction Rub.** Muffled heart sounds if effusion. Absent lung findings unless right-sided failure develops. | Signs of ischemia (S1/S2 changes). Crackles if LV failure. |\n| **ECG Pattern** | Diffuse ST-segment elevation (concave up) in multiple leads (except aVR, V1). | Localized ST-elevation (STEMI) or ST-depression (NSTEMI). ST changes follow coronary vascular territory. |\n\n*Check:* While Pericarditis has a friction rub, the specificity decreases in the elderly, those on beta-blockers (masking rubs), or obese patients. Conversely, MI pain can become pleuritic if significant pericardial irritation ensues post-infarction.\n\n### 3. Pulmonary Embolism (PE) vs. ACS\n\nDifferentiation relies heavily on respiratory status and hemodynamics.\n\n| Feature | ACS (Ischemic) | Pulmonary Embolism (PE) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Primary Sensation** | Throat/chest wall discomfort (Pressure). | Respiratory distress (air hunger), sharp chest pain. |\n| **Respiratory Rate** | Usually normal unless concurrent HF. | Tachypneic (often $>20$ bpm). |\n| **Hemodynamics** | Systolic BP may drop if massive occlusion, but often stable until decompensation. | Tachycardia almost universal ($>100$ bpm). Pulse oximetry usually low. |\n| **Lung Exam** | Often normal (unless concomitant HF/effusion). | Can be normal, or wheezing ("pepper pot"), absent sounds in large emboli. |\n| **Associated Signs** | Diaphoresis, elevated enzymes. | Hypoxia, Cretan leg swelling (source limb), pleural rub. |\n| **Mechanism** | Demand-supply imbalance. | Obstructive vascular occlusion $\\rightarrow$ alveolar dead space $\\rightarrow$ hypoxic drive. |\n\n*Note:* PE presents with dyspnea more than chest pain. The presence of chest pain without hypoxia does not exclude PE, nor does pain location confirm or deny it. Wells Criteria and D-dimer levels are adjuncts here.\n\n### 4. Aortic Dissection vs. Other Cardiac Causes\n\nThis is the most dangerous mimic due to rapid mortality if missed.\n\n| Feature | Dissection | Angina (Typical/Atypical) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Onset** | Sudden, catastrophic ("tearing"). Occurs over seconds/minutes. | Gradual (minutes/hours). Related to exertion or stress. |\n| **Quality** | Ripping, splitting, tearing sensation. | Heavy, crushing, squeezing. |\n| **Radiation** | **Jaw, arms, flank, supraclavicular region, interscapular area.** | Typically unilateral arm/neck, jaw, back. |\n| **Vitals** | Pulse pressure narrowing. Intermittent pulse deficits. | Normal BP, unless shock. |\n| **Physical Exam** | **Discrepancy in blood pressures between upper and lower extremities.** Weak femoral pulses. Neck bruising. | Normal pulses. Murmurs of valvular disease possible (AS). |\n| **Complications** | Stroke, organ malperfusion, rupture. | Arrhythmias, failure, death. |\n\n*Critical Clue:* If the patient has **new-onset syncope** with chest pain, **hypotension** despite treatment, or **pulse deficits**, Dissection must be assumed until proven otherwise.\n\n### 5. Musculoskeletal vs. Referred Pain\n\nThese are common non-life-threatening causes requiring ruling out above first.\n\n| Feature | Musculoskeletal | Pericarditis / Cardiac |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Trigger** | Movement of chest wall (deep inhalation, cough). | Emotional stress, exertion, deep breathing. |\n| **Reproducibility** | **Positive.** Tenderness upon direct palpation of the rib/sternum. | Negative. No specific point tenderness on skin. |\n| **Timing** | Morning stiffness or late night pain. | Post-prandial (GI) or positional (cardiac). |\n| **Location** | Anterior chest (clavicle, sternum, ribs). | Mid-sternal or retrosternal. |\n| **Resolution** | Improves with NSAIDs/Rest. | Improves with Nitrates (Cardiac) or Forward lean (Pericarditis). |\n\n*Validation:* Direct palpation reproducing the exact pain site is highly specific for musculoskeletal. However, inflammation of the costochondral junctions (Tietze syndrome) can mimic pericardial irritation.\n\n### Final Assessment\n\nIn clinical practice, **clinical judgment must align with objective data**. While the above patterns provide differentiation, several caveats apply:\n1.  **Overlap:** Dissection can present with stable vitals initially. ACS can cause pleuritic pain if pericardial effusion forms (peri-myocarditis). Pericarditis can present with ST elevations mimicking STEMI but in diffuse areas.\n2.  **Risk Stratification:** Never rely solely on bedside discrimination for life-threatening conditions. Always treat suspected ACS/Dissection with broad-spectrum coverage (nitrates, anticoagulation) and imaging (CT Angio) regardless of exam findings if suspicion remains high.\n3.  **Population Specifics:** Elderly patients often have silent ischemia or atypical presentations (diarrhea/abdominal pain) masking the underlying pathology.\n\n```'}]}
