ceed with the plan:\n1. Define assumptions.\n2. Derive Likelihood.\n3. MLE.\n4. MAP (with prior).\n5. Bayesian (posterior).\n6. Summary.\n\nLet\'s refine the constraints.\n"Finish thinking with exactly one native  [something] boundary."\nThe instruction text provided in the system message is: "Finish thinking with exactly one native \\n\\n\\begin{verbatim}...\\end{verbatim}".\nThis is ambiguous. I will follow the standard behavior: Think internally, output the final answer.\nI will ensure the final answer is clean and standalone.\nI will check if there are any "false premises" in the user request. User asks for "MLE vs MAP vs full Bayesian". This is standard.\nUser asks to "Walk through". I will provide derivations.\nUser asks to "See all three side by side". I will structure the answer to highlight them.\n\nLet\'s focus on the math.\nLikelihood: $p(D|\\mu) = \\prod (2\\pi\\sigma^2)^{-1/2} \\exp(-(x_i-\\mu)^2 / 2\\sigma^2)$.\nLog-Likelihood: $-\\frac{n}{2}\\ln(2\\pi\\sigma^2) - \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\sum (x_i-\\mu)^2$.\nOptimize w.r.t $\\mu$: $\\frac{d}{d\\mu} (\\mu^2) = 0 \\implies \\hat{\\mu}_{MLE} = \\bar{x}$.\n\nPrior (for MAP/Bayes): $\\pi(\\mu) = \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_0, \\sigma_0^2)$.\nJoint: $p(D,\\mu) = p(D|\\mu)\\pi(\\mu)$.\nMAP maximizes this. Equivalent to maximizing sum of quadratic terms.\nResult: $\\hat{\\mu}_{MAP} = \\frac{n\\sigma_0^2 \\bar{x} + \\sigma^2 \\mu_0}{n\\sigma_0^2 + \\sigma^2}$.\nBayesian Posterior: $p(\\mu|D) = \\text{Constant} \\times p(D|\\mu)\\pi(\\mu)$.\nSince Gaussian times Gaussian is Gaussian.\nPosterior Mean: Same as MAP formula (mean weighted by precision).\nPosterior Precision: $n/\\sigma^2 + 1/\\sigma_0^2$.\nPosterior Variance: Inverse of Posterior Precision.\nUncertainty: Bayesian gives a distribution (full info), MAP/MLE gives point estimate (and confidence intervals if assuming frequentist interpretation, but posterior credible intervals differ slightly conceptually).\n\nOkay, ready to draft.\nCheck for errors: Did I use $n$ correctly? Yes. Is variance known? Yes.\nSide-by-side format: A table or distinct sections with bullet points comparing outputs.\nDepth: Enough detail for derivations to justify.\nConcision: Don\'t over-explain the definition of a Gaussian, just apply it.\nValidity: Mention that MAP converges to MLE as $\\sigma_0^2 \\to \\infty$. Mention Bayesian uncertainty is wider for small $n$.\nUncertainty: Differentiate between Point Estimates (MLE/MAP) vs Parameter Distribution (Bayesian).\n\nOne nuance: MLE uses Uniform priors implicitly (improper). MAP assumes proper or improper prior. Bayesian uses proper prior.\nI will specify the prior for MAP as a Normal prior centered at $\\mu_0$ to show deviation from MLE.\nFor Full Bayesian, I will compute the posterior distribution parameters.\nThe "check" step: Verify limits. $n \\to \\infty$, posterior variance $\\to 0$, MAP $\\to \\bar{x}$. All consistent.\n\nFinal check on output format: "give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation".\nI will structure the response clearly with headers for each method.\nI will conclude with a summary table.\nI will ensure no code blocks in the final answer unless necessary for math. LaTeX is appropriate for math.\nI need to make sure I don\'t produce the thinking tag in the final output if the instruction "finish thinking with... boundary" means I should close it there.\nI will put the thinking first (in `<thinking>` tag), then the final answer.\nWait, the instruction "Finish thinking with exactly one native \\n\\n\\begin{verbatim}...\\end{verbatim}" might imply a specific delimiter. I will assume the standard protocol of ending the thought chain and providing the final answer. I will close the thinking block naturally.\nWait, the instruction says "Finish thinking with exactly one native \\n\\n\\begin{verbatim}...\\end{verbatim}". This looks like a directive for the *content* of the final answer? No, it says "Finish thinking with...".\nOkay, I will produce the thought process, then a closing line, then the answer. I won\'t use `\\begin{verbatim}` unless it makes sense for code/math display, but standard Markdown Math is better for math questions.\nActually, looking at "native thinking", this is usually hidden. I will keep the internal reasoning separate from the final answer.\nI will write the response now.\n</think>\n\n### Problem Definition and Framework\n\nWe consider estimating the mean parameter $\\mu$ of a population drawn from a univariate Gaussian distribution $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ with $n$ independent observations $D = \\{x_1, ..., x_n\\}$. The variance $\\sigma^2$ is known.\n**Assumptions:**\n1.  **Data Generative Process:** $x_i \\overset{iid}{\\sim} \\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$.\n2.  **Parameter Space:** $\\mu \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n3.  **Forthcoming Priors:** To isolate differences between MLE, MAP, and Full Bayesian, we will introduce a proper Gaussian prior $\\pi(\\mu) = \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_0, \\sigma_0^2)$ for the MAP and Bayesian sections. MLE corresponds to the limit of a flat (uniform) prior.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Likelihood Construction\n\nThe likelihood function $L(\\mu | D) = p(D | \\mu)$ describes the probability of observing the data given the parameter.\n\n$$ p(D | \\mu) = \\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi\\sigma^2}} \\exp\\left( -\\frac{(x_i - \\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2} \\right) $$\n\nTaking the natural logarithm (log-likelihood $\\ell(\\mu)$) simplifies the product to a sum and isolates the dependence on $\\mu$:\n\n$$ \\ell(\\mu) = -\\frac{n}{2}\\ln(2\\pi\\sigma^2) - \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\mu)^2 $$\n\nTo simplify calculation, we substitute $S_\\mu = \\sum x_i$. Note that minimizing $\\sum (x_i - \\mu)^2$ is equivalent to finding the value closest to the centroid. The term involving $n$ and $\\sigma^2$ is positive, so maximizing $\\ell(\\mu)$ minimizes the quadratic loss.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE)\n\nMLE seeks the $\\hat{\\mu}_{MLE}$ that maximizes $L(\\mu | D)$ (or $\\ell(\\mu)$) without considering any prior information about $\\mu$.\n\n**Derivation:**\nTake the derivative of $\\ell(\\mu)$ with respect to $\\mu$ and set to zero:\n$$ \\frac{d\\ell}{d\\mu} = -\\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\sum_{i=1}^n 2(x_i - \\mu)(-1) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma^2} \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\mu) $$\n\nSet equal to zero:\n$$ \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = n \\mu \\implies \\hat{\\mu}_{MLE} = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = \\bar{x} $$\n\n**Result:**\n$$ \\hat{\\mu}_{MLE} = \\bar{x}, \\quad \\text{Variance of estimator} \\approx \\sigma^2/n $$\nMLE provides the sample mean and yields no inherent uncertainty weight (treats $\\mu$ as unconstrained).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Maximum A Posteriori (MAP)\n\nMAP finds the mode of the posterior distribution by combining the likelihood and a prior belief $\\pi(\\mu)$ via Bayes\' Theorem ($p(\\mu|D) \\propto p(D|\\mu)\\pi(\\mu)$). Here we define an informative prior $\\pi(\\mu) = \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_0, \\sigma_0^2)$ to create a contrast with MLE.\n\n**Derivation:**\nMaximize $\\log(p(D|\\mu)\\pi(\\mu))$:\n$$ \\log(\\text{Posterior}) \\propto \\underbrace{-\\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\sum (x_i - \\mu)^2}_{\\text{Log Likelihood}} - \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2\\sigma_0^2}(\\mu - \\mu_0)^2}_{\\text{Log Prior}} $$\nDifferentiating w.r.t $\\mu$ and setting to zero:\n$$ \\frac{1}{\\sigma^2}(n\\mu - \\sum x_i) - \\frac{1}{\\sigma_0^2}(\\mu - \\mu_0) = 0 $$\n$$ \\mu \\left( \\frac{n}{\\sigma^2} + \\frac{1}{\\sigma_0^2} \\right) = \\frac{\\bar{x} n}{\\sigma^2} + \\frac{\\mu_0}{\\sigma_0^2} $$\n\nSolving for $\\mu$:\n$$ \\hat{\\mu}_{MAP} = \\frac{\\frac{n}{\\sigma^2} + \\frac{1}{\\sigma_0^2}}{\\frac{n}{\\sigma^2} + \\frac{1}{\\sigma_0^2}} \\cdot \\text{weighted avg} $$\n$$ \\hat{\\mu}_{MAP} = \\frac{n\\sigma_0^2 \\bar{x} + \\sigma^2 \\mu_0}{n\\sigma_0^2 + \\sigma^2} $$\n\n**Result:**\nMAP shrinks the estimate toward the prior mean $\\mu_0$. If $\\sigma_0^2$ is large (weak prior), the shrinkage is minimal. If $\\sigma_0^2$ is small (strong prior), $\\hat{\\mu}_{MAP} \\to \\mu_0$.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Full Bayesian Inference\n\nBayesian inference computes the entire posterior distribution $p(\\mu | D)$ rather than a point estimate. Since the likelihood is Gaussian and the prior is Gaussian (conjugate family), the posterior is also Gaussian.\n\n**Derivation:**\nThe posterior density takes the form $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu_{post}, \\sigma_{post}^2)$ where the parameters combine precisions (inverse variances):\n$$ \\text{Precision}_{post} = \\text{Precision}_{data} + \\text{Precision}_{prior} = \\frac{n}{\\sigma^2} + \\frac{1}{\\sigma_0^2} $$\n$$ \\sigma_{post}^2 = \\left( \\frac{n}{\\sigma^2} + \\frac{1}{\\sigma_0^2} \\right)^{-1} = \\frac{\\sigma^2 \\sigma_0^2}{n\\sigma_0^2 + \\sigma^2} $$\nThe posterior mean matches the MAP estimate:\n$$ \\mu_{post} = \\text{Precision}_{data}^{-1} \\bar{x} + \\text{Precision}_{prior}^{-1} \\mu_0 $$\n\n**Result:**\n$$ p(\\mu | D) = \\mathcal{N}(\\hat{\\mu}_{MAP}, \\sigma_{post}^2) $$\nUnlike MLE/MAP, the Full Bayesian approach provides a **distributional measure of uncertainty**. One can derive a Credible Interval (e.g., 95% CI) directly from the posterior variance, which incorporates both data noise and prior knowledge uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n### Side-by-Side Comparison\n\n| Feature | Maximum Likelihood (MLE) | Maximum A Posteriori (MAP) | Full Bayesian |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Objective** | Maximize Likelihood $L(\\mu|D)$ | Maximize Posterior Mode $p(D,\\mu)$ | Integrate Over $p(\\mu|D)$ |\n| **Point Estimate** | $\\hat{\\mu}_{MLE} = \\bar{x}$ | $\\hat{\\mu}_{MAP} = \\frac{n\\sigma_0^2 \\bar{x} + \\sigma^2 \\mu_0}{n\\sigma_0^2 + \\sigma^2}$ | Same as MAP |\n| **Estimate Dependence** | Depends only on Data | Data + Prior Mean $\\mu_0$ | Data + Prior Mean $\\mu_0$ |\n| **Uncertainty Handling** | Fails to quantify estimation error (requires resampling/CIs separately) | Only quantifies error in $\\sigma^2$ (if modeled); ignores prior uncertainty | Provides full Credible Interval; reflects prior uncertainty |\n| **Limit Behavior** | N/A | $\\to \\bar{x}$ as $\\sigma_0^2 \\to \\infty$ (Flat Prior) | Converges to Delta at $\\bar{x}$ as $\\sigma_0^2 \\to \\infty$ |\n| **Computation Cost** | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ (Closed-form) |\n\n### Validation and Limits\n\n*   **Agreement Condition:** When the prior variance $\\sigma_0^2 \\to \\infty$ (uniform/uninformative prior), the MAP estimate recovers the MLE estimate ($\\hat{\\mu}_{MAP} = \\bar{x}$). Thus, MLE is the limiting case of MAP with a flat prior.\n*   **Large Sample Limit:** As $n \\to \\infty$, the posterior variance $\\sigma_{post}^2 \\to 0$ because the data precision $n/\\sigma^2$ dominates the prior precision $1/\\sigma_0^2$. Consequently, the posterior becomes a delta function around the true parameter $\\mu$, and all methods converge to the true mean.\n*   **Conceptual Distinction:** MLE treats the probability space as $p(D|\\mu)$. MAP treats the probability space as a trade-off between $p(D|\\mu)$ and beliefs $\\pi(\\mu)$. Full Bayesian integrates $p(\\theta, D)$ to update the probability of $\\mu$ itself, offering direct probabilistic answers ("There is 95% probability $\\mu$ lies in range X").'}]}
