ta_o(\\text{NH}_3)$ for Co(III) $\\approx$ 23,000 cm$^{-1}$.\n        *   $\\Delta_o(\\text{Cl}^-)$ for Co(III) $\\approx$ 18,000 cm$^{-1}$.\n        *   Wait, let\'s verify the range.\n        *   Co(II) aqua: ~9,300 cm-1 (HS). $P$ is usually around 20k cm-1. So $9300 < 20000 \\rightarrow$ HS.\n        *   Co(III) aqua: $\\approx$ 18,000 cm-1 (Wait, Co(III) aqua is actually low spin). Let me check the value for Co(III) hexaaqua. Often it\'s around 18,000 cm-1. If $P$ is ~22,000 cm-1... wait, if $\\Delta_o <$ $P$, shouldn\'t it be HS? Co(III) [aq] is unstable and oxidizes to O2 quickly. The most common stable Co(III) complex is with N-donors like NH3 or CN-.\n        *   Correction: Co(III) hexaaqua exists transiently? No, Co(III) is kinetically inert. Common Co(III) is [Co(NH3)6]3+. $\\Delta_o$ for NH3 is ~22,800 cm-1. $P$ is typically ~21,000 cm-1. So $\\Delta_o > P \\rightarrow$ LS.\n        *   Is there a Co(III) HS example? Very rare. High spin Co(III) implies instability or specific constraints. But the rule holds.\n        *   Co(II) HS: [Co(H2O)6]2+ is ~10,000 cm-1. Definitely HS.\n        *   Co(II) LS: [Co(CN)6]4- is strong field. $\\Delta_o$ > 30,000 cm-1. Definitely LS.\n        *   $P$ estimation: For first-row transition metals, $P$ is often approximated at 20,000-25,000 cm-1 (depending on ion size/charge). Since Co(III) pulls orbitals closer, $\\Delta_o$ increases faster than $P$.\n\n    *   *Check Validity:* Is Co(III) *always* low spin? Technically no, but "almost always" covers the vast majority of stable isolable compounds (e.g., in aqueous solution, solid salts). High-spin Co(III) is extremely rare (usually only in distorted geometries or with very weak ligands, which often lead to oxidation). The prompt accepts "almost always", so I don\'t need to argue against it unless it\'s factually wrong. I will support the premise.\n    *   *Check $d^7$ case:* Co(II) is $d^7$.\n        *   HS: $t_{2g}^5 e_g^2$. Magnetic moment $\\mu \\approx 4.9$ BM.\n    