e system\'s specific syntax.\n    I will use `</thought>` to demarcate the reasoning part clearly, as that is standard for "thought process" vs "answer".\n    Wait, "Finish thinking with exactly one native `</think>` boundary."\n    Could it mean I should literally write `</thought>`? Yes.\n    Okay.\n\n    Final check on content:\n    Make sure not to say "I think". Say "Consider..." or direct explanation.\n    Ensure I clarify "Left Eigenvector" vs "Right Eigenvector".\n    Transition matrix $P$: $P_{ij} = P(X_{t+1}=j | X_t=i)$. Row sums to 1.\n    Distribution $\\pi$: Row vector. $\\pi(t+1) = \\pi(t) P$.\n    Stationary: $\\pi P = \\pi$.\n    Why intuition fails: People expect $Px = \\lambda x$ (column vector).\n    Correction: Probabilities transform as row vectors.\n    Analogy: Water tank levels.\n    Stability: Eigenvalues $< 1$ mean transients fade.\n\n    Okay, let\'s write.\n</think>\n\nTo understand the stationary distribution intuitively, consider the physical meaning of a Markov chain not as random jumps, but as a flow of probability mass through a system.\n\n**1. Conservation Law (The Flow Balance)**\nImagine the states of the Markov chain as different rooms, and $P_{ij}$ as the rate at which people move from room $i$ to room $j$. The stationary distribution $\\pi$ describes the fraction of people occupying each room once the system has stabilized.\n\nIf you are in a stable configuration (stationary), the number of people entering room $i$ must exactly equal the number of people leaving room $i$.\n$$ \\text{Total Inflow}_i = \\text{Total Outflow}_i $$\nThis balance condition is precisely what defines the stationary equations. If you express the total probability in a system at time $t$ as a row vector $\\pi^{(t)}$, one step of evolution shifts this vector by applying the transition matrix $P$. For the distribution to remain unchanged, the shift operation must be the identity.\n$$ \\pi P = \\pi $$\nMathematically, this means $\\pi$ is an eigenvector of $P$ with eigenvalue 1. The phrase "left eigenvector" arises because probability distributions (as sets of weights) sit on the left side of the matrix multiplication $\\pi P$, whereas "right eigenvectors" $v P = \\lambda v$ are used when tracking single-particle trajectories (columns summing to 1 in column-stochastic matrices).\n\n**2. Why Eigenvalue 1 Matters (The Spectrum)**\nConsider the transition matrix $P$ decomposed via its eigenvectors and eigenvalues. For an irreducible finite chain, 1 is guaranteed to be an eigenvalue. All other eigenvalues $\\lambda_k$ satisfy $|\\lambda_k| < 1$ (in the ergodic case).\nAny initial probability distribution $\\pi^{(0)}$ can be written as a linear combination of these eigenvectors:\n$$ \\pi^{(0)} = c_0 \\mathbf{v}_0 + \\sum_{k=1}^{N} c_k \\mathbf{v}_k $$\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_0$ corresponds to eigenvalue 1 (the stationary mode). When evolving the system forward in time:\n$$ \\pi^{(t)} = \\pi^{(0)} P^t = c_0 \\mathbf{v}_0 + \\sum_{k=1}^{N} c_k \\lambda_k^t \\mathbf{v}_k $$\nSince $|\\lambda_k| < 1$, the terms $\\lambda_k^t$ decay toward zero as $t \\to \\infty$. The transient components vanish, leaving only $c_0 \\mathbf{v}_0$. The system naturally relaxes onto the specific linear combination corresponding to eigenvalue 1. The "1" eigenvalue represents the conserved mass that does not decay or grow over time; the rest are "transient noise" that eventually dissipates.\n\n**3. Normalization**\nBecause probability mass is conserved ($\\sum \\pi_i = 1$), the eigenvector associated with eigenvalue 1 cannot just be any scalar multiple of itself—it must be scaled such that its entries sum to 1. Thus, the stationary distribution is the unique unnormalized eigenvector (for irreducible chains) normalized to unit mass.\n\nIn summary, the stationary distribution is the eigenvector with eigenvalue 1 because it is the only mode that resists decay under repeated application of the transition rules.'}]}
