olumn. Seafloor GPS stations (deployed by JAMSTEC) recorded 24-31 meters of horizontal displacement and 3-5 meters of vertical uplift near the trench. Ocean-bottom pressure gauges captured the initial wave, which exceeded 2 meters amplitude in the open ocean — extraordinary for a tsunami source.\n\n**The Jogan precedent, ignored.** Geological investigations by Minoura et al. (2001) had documented sand sheets from the 869 CE Jogan tsunami extending 3-4 km inland on the Sendai plain — far beyond the inundation zones used in contemporary hazard planning. This evidence was acknowledged but not incorporated into the official tsunami hazard maps, which were based on the largest instrumentally recorded event in the region (the 1933 Sanriku M8.4).\n\n**Implications for early warning.** Post-Tohoku, Japan overhauled its tsunami warning system to incorporate real-time offshore observations (DONET and S-net cabled observatories) rather than relying solely on seismic magnitude proxies. The broader lesson — that shallow megathrust slip can dramatically amplify tsunami generation beyond what magnitude alone predicts — has reshaped hazard assessments in Cascadia, Chile, and the Sunda Trench.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_5191', 'source_prompt_hash': '899d41b7bd9b2684fd9020d77ce833e6a6af17e65a6133d516385c263f18a299', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 445, 'reference_tokens': 566, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider