gnature and extracted key parameters: the 3.4 angstrom rise per base pair and the 34 angstrom helix repeat, implying 10 bases per turn. Separately, Watson and Crick had access to a Medical Research Council report containing Franklin's precise crystallographic measurements — again, shared through channels she was unaware of.\n\nThe final piece was Chargaff's rules: Erwin Chargaff had shown that in any DNA sample, the amount of adenine equals thymine and guanine equals cytosine. Watson and Crick initially did not appreciate the significance of this, but once they recognized that A-T and G-C base pairs had identical geometries (same width), the double helix with complementary base pairing fell into place. Jerry Donohue, an office mate, corrected Watson on the tautomeric forms of the bases — a chemical detail that was essential for getting the hydrogen bonding right.\n\nThe resulting Nature paper (April 25, 1953) is famously understated. Franklin and Wilkins published companion papers in the same issue, but the framing positioned their work as supporting evidence for Watson and Crick's model, rather than as the foundational data that made the model possible.\n\nFranklin died of ovarian cancer in 1958, possibly related to her extensive X-ray exposure. The Nobel Prize was awarded in 1962 to Watson, Crick, and Wilkins. Nobel rules prohibit posthumous awards, but Watson's memoir The Double Helix (1968) compounded the erasure by portraying Franklin dismissively, focusing on her appearance and temperament rather than her scientific contributions.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_7111', 'source_prompt_hash': 'f5b9149c01effaa32d515034533f3dae9df6c199f06cc3f86a3c1cbdb39ab60b', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 461, 'reference_tokens': 612, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier as