 56%|█████▌    | 96/171 [4:39:02<3:36:33, 173.24s/it][RANK 0] All reward functions returned None for the following kwargs:
{'conversation_id': 'conv_5889', 'example_id': 'conv_5889_message_2', 'category': 'science', 'prompt_hash': 'ab786dde132683d08495f17de91579751d29e56a4449f8e34a0808392d6ae1b3', 'user_question': 'How do cancer cells evade the immune system through immune checkpoint mechanisms?', 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a science and technology educator. Make complex topics accessible.'}], 'reference_answer': "Cancer immune evasion through checkpoint mechanisms is one of the most important discoveries in modern oncology, revealing how tumors co-opt the immune system's own regulatory machinery to shield themselves from destruction.\n\nUnder normal immunosurveillance, the immune system effectively detects and eliminates nascent cancers. Tumor cells accumulate mutations that produce neoantigens — abnormal proteins absent from normal tissue. Dendritic cells engulf tumor debris, process these neoantigens, and present them to T cells in lymph nodes. With proper costimulation (TCR/MHC signal plus CD28/B7 engagement plus IL-12), tumor-specific CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes are activated, traffic to the tumor, recognize neoantigens displayed on tumor MHC class I molecules, and kill through perforin/granzyme mechanisms. This system works — the dramatically elevated cancer rates in immunocompromised patients demonstrate its importance.\n\nThe PD-1/PD-L1 axis is the most clinically significant evasion mechanism. PD-1 is an inhibitory receptor expressed on activated T cells that normally functions to limit immune responses and prevent autoimmunity. Its ligand PD-L1 is expressed at low levels on various normal tissues. Cancer cells exploit this by massively upregulating PD-L1 through two routes: adaptive resistance, where interferon-gamma secreted by infiltrating T cells paradoxically induces PD-L1 expression on the tumor cells they are trying to kill, and oncogenic signaling, where pathways involving PTEN loss, PI3K/Akt activation, ALK rearrangements, or MYC amplification drive constitutive PD-L1 transcription. When PD-L1 on the tumor engages PD-1 on the T cell, SHP-2 phosphatase is recruited and dephosphorylates critical TCR signaling molecules, rendering the T cell anergic and exhausted — alive but functionally paralyzed.\n\nThe CTLA-4 pathway operates at an earlier stage, primarily in lymph nodes during T cell priming. CTLA-4 competes with the costimulatory receptor CD28 for binding to B7 ligands on antigen-presenting cells, and binds with approximately 20-fold higher affinity. CTLA-4 engagement delivers an inhibitory signal and physically sequesters B7, preventing the costimulation required for full T cell activation. Regulatory T cells constitutively express CTLA-4 and accumulate in tumors, where they suppress anti-tumor immunity. Tumors actively recruit Tregs through CCL22 secretion.\n\nBeyond checkpoints, cancers deploy additional evasion strategies: downregulating MHC class I through beta-2-microglobulin mutations to become invisible to CD8+ T cells, recruiting myeloid-derived suppressor cells and M2-polarized macrophages, secreting immunosuppressive factors including TGF-beta, IL-10, and the tryptophan-catabolizing enzyme IDO, and creating a hostile tumor microenvironment characterized by hypoxia, acidosis, and nutrient depletion.\n\nThe framework of cancer immunoediting, proposed by Robert Schreiber, captures the dynamic relationship: during elimination, immunogenic tumor cells are destroyed; during equilibrium, the immune system contains but cannot eradicate the tumor, creating selection pressure; and during escape, resistant clones emerge bearing checkpoint ligands, lacking MHC expression, and cultivating immunosuppressive microenvironments. Checkpoint inhibitor therapy — anti-PD-1 (nivolumab, pembrolizumab), anti-PD-L1 (atezolizumab), anti-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab) — works by releasing the brakes that tumors have commandeered, restoring the T cell's ability to recognize and destroy cancer.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_5889', 'source_prompt_hash': 'c99e7b4eb1a7874aba2948b1d7488f61980f5d42b2b27a102d25df80c37b53b4', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 424, 'reference_tokens': 757, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not