*\nState that light travels faster than sound. Give approximate speeds. Mention time delay calculation. Keep it simple.\n\n*Constraint Check:* "Finish thinking with exactly one native `\n</think>` boundary."\nSo the very last thing in the reasoning block should be that string.\nThen the final answer starts.\nI will format it as:\n[Thinking content...]\n```\n</think>\n[Answer content...]\nActually, I shouldn\'t use code blocks around `\n</think>`. Just the string.\nOkay, ready.\n\n*Wait, I need to ensure I don\'t accidentally start a thought block.*\nThe prompt says "The chat template may already open the native thinking channel".\nI should avoid triggering another one.\nI will just write the text.\nOkay, proceeding to generation.\n\n*Wait, re-reading "Do not invent sources..."*\nFact: Light speed in air ~ c/1.0003. Negligible difference for this context.\nSound speed ~ 343 m/s.\nCalculation: For 1 km, light takes ~3 microseconds, sound takes ~3 seconds.\nDelay ~ 3 seconds per km (or 5 seconds per mile).\nCorrect.\n\n*Final Polish:* Ensure no markdown headers. Just prose. Ensure the `\n</think>` boundary is present.\n\n*Wait, does the instruction imply I should NOT output the thinking?*\n"In your native thinking... Finish thinking with exactly one native `\n</think>` boundary."\nUsually, when told to use "native thinking" in this manner, the intention is that the model should produce the thinking internally (hidden) and then the answer.\nHOWEVER, the prompt also says "Do not add special XML-style process or answer tags" but then asks to finish thinking with a boundary.\nIf I output the thinking visibly, it might violate "don\'t add special tags".\nBUT, "Finish thinking with... `\n</think>`" implies the thinking IS being written out to reach that boundary.\nIf the thinking was hidden, I wouldn\'t "finish thinking" in the output.\nGiven the explicit instruction to write the boundary `\n</think>` in the output (as a text string to mark the end of the thought section), I