"Do not add special XML-style... tags"? Using `\n</think>` is explicitly allowed/implied by the boundary instruction.\nOkay.\n\n(Final Plan):\nText block of reasoning.\nBlank line.\nToken `\n</think>`.\nBlank line (optional, but clearer).\nFinal answer text block.\n\nWait, "Finish thinking with exactly one native `\n</think>` boundary."\nDoes that mean the LAST character of my output should be the last char of `\n</think>`?\nNo, "Finish thinking with" implies the thinking portion ends there. The final answer comes after.\nOkay.\n\nLet\'s write.\n\nThinking Trace:\n1.  Analyze the phenomenon: Lightning produces both electromagnetic radiation and acoustic waves simultaneously.\n2.  Identify the mediums: Air (and vacuum between ionized regions).\n3.  Apply constants: $c \\approx 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s for light. $v_{air} \\approx 343$ m/s for sound.\n4.  Calculate comparison: Light arrives $\\sim 1,000,000$ times faster.\n5.  Determine temporal effect: At 1km distance, light reaches in microseconds, sound reaches in seconds.\n6.  Conclusion: Delay is due to speed differential.\n7.  Verify assumptions: Refractive index of air affects light negligibly for this scale. Temperature affects sound speed slightly but doesn\'t change order of magnitude.\n8.  Draft final answer.\n9.  Review against "concise" and "correct" guidelines.\n\nOkay, writing response.\n</think>\n\nLightning generates both light (electromagnetic radiation) and sound (acoustic pressure waves) simultaneously within the atmosphere. While both travel toward you from the cloud ground interaction, they propagate at vastly different speeds through air.\n\nLight travels at approximately $300,000$ kilometers per second ($3 \\times 10^8$ m/s), which effectively appears instantaneous over human-scale distances. Sound travels roughly $343$ meters per second (dependent on temperature and humidity). Consequently, even though the sound is generated by the rapid heating and expansion of air caused by the lightning (creating the thunder), the visual information from the electrical discharge reaches your eyes almost immediately, while the acoustic energy lags significantly behind. Over a distance of one kilometer, you will perceive the light first, with the thunder arriving roughly three seconds later.'}]}
