 technical reasons.\n3.  Ensure the final answer is standalone.\n\n**Constraint Checklist & Confidence Score:**\n1.  Native thinking trace? Yes.\n2.  Finish with one native boundary? Yes.\n3.  Self-contained final answer? Yes.\n4.  No XML in final answer? Yes.\n5.  No personal experience? Yes.\n6.  Check consistency/alternatives? Yes.\n\nConfidence Score: 100%.\n\nMental Sandbox Simulation:\n*   Input: User doubts "weakly informative" priors.\n*   Draft: Explain "weakly" vs "strong". Compare to frequentist shrinkage. Emphasize transparency.\n*   Safety: Is discussing Bayesian priors risky? No.\n*   Format: Thinking block (hidden or shown?), then Answer.\n*   Correction: The instruction says "do not open it again". If the platform expects me to hide the thinking, I should just think internally. However, the instruction also says "use a natural thinking trace". This usually means "write the thinking". I\'ll write a short section labeled "Reasoning" then "Final Answer".\n*   Wait, the instruction says "Finish thinking with exactly one native boundary". This might mean a triple newline.\n*   Okay, I will write the thinking block then a triple newline then the answer.\n\nWait, I need to check if I should reveal the thinking to the user. "The chat template may already open the native thinking channel... do not open it again". This implies there IS a channel. If I\'m the model generating text, I usually can\'t control UI visibility of thinking unless specified. I will assume the role is to *generate* the reasoning text as part of the output if the prompt structure requires it, or keep it hidden. Given "use a natural thinking trace... do not add special XML-style process or answer tags", I should probably write the thinking text as the first part of the response, but the "final answer" should be clean.\nActually, standard practice for these specific instructions (often seen in "System Instructions" for high-quality models) is to output the thinking *inside* a specific block that the user might see, OR the instruction is for *internal* reasoning.\n"If the chat template may already open... do not open it again." This suggests I am *allowed* to output thinking if it\'s already open. I will output a "Reasoning" section, then the Final Answer.\nWait, I\'ll try to be concise. I will output a summary of reasoning, then the answer.\nActually, the safest path to "Native Thinking" compliance is to write the thinking first.\nOkay.\n\n**Refined Plan:**\n1.  Write a section analyzing the ethical implications and the definition of "weakly informative".\n2.  Write a section comparing Bayesian regularization to frequentist methods.\n3.  Write a section on best practices (transparency).\n4.  Add a separation line (native boundary).\n5.  Write the final answer clearly.\n\n**Wait**, "Finish thinking with exactly one native boundary." Usually, this means the last token of the reasoning is followed by `\\n\\n\\n`. Then the Final Answer starts.\nI will adhere to this strict formatting.\n\n**Content:**\n*   Validate the suspicion (it\'s intuitive).\n*   Define Weakly Informative (constraints on parameter space, not outcome prediction).\n*   Contrast with Informative (outcome prediction).\n*   Analogy: Lasso/Ridge.\n*   Ethical stance: Transparency.\n\n**Execution:**\nI\'ll draft the thinking section first. Then the final answer.\nI need to make sure I don\'t violate "no quotes of instructions".\nOkay, ready.\n\nOne more check: "Do not invent sources". I won\'t cite Cohen (1994) again or Bürkner unless necessary. I\'ll rely on general consensus.\nActually, I mentioned Cohen earlier in the conversation context (I see the conversation history includes "classic Cohen (1994) paper"). I should maintain consistency with the established tone of the interaction (since the conversation history is provided as context to me, though it wasn\'t generated by me).\nWait, the prompt says "User\'s actual question... Earlier assistant statements... can be mistaken... identify