{'conversation_id': 'conv_493', 'example_id': 'conv_493_message_2', 'category': 'earth_science', 'prompt_hash': 'db262895418e8db5930f2b74ea224f58fc94e7f2de67a1f1ffe6b1efde35a372', 'user_question': "Marine ice sheet instability gets thrown around a lot in Antarctic papers but I want the actual mechanics. Why is a reverse-sloped bed unstable, what's the flux-thickness relation, and is the MISI framework still holding up given the MICI debate?", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a glaciologist specializing in West Antarctic ice dynamics. Write for a climate scientist who understands mass balance but is fuzzy on the details of marine ice sheet instability.'}], 'reference_answer': 'The short answer: MISI is a physically robust instability mechanism that unambiguously applies to much of West Antarctica; MICI is a distinct, more speculative hypothesis that may or may not be important. They\'re often conflated. Let me separate them.\n\n**Flux-thickness relation at the grounding line**\n\nSchoof (2007, JGR) derived the grounding-line flux for a marine ice sheet in plug-flow approximation with a Coulomb-or-power-law sliding at the bed. The result, for a Weertman sliding law τ_b = C u^{1/m} and Glen rheology with exponent n:\n\nq_g ∝ [ρ_i g (1 − ρ_i/ρ_w) ]^{(m+n+3)/(m+1)} · h_g^{(m+n+3)/(m+1)} · (other constants)\n\nFor n = 3, m = 1/3 (a rough Coulomb limit) you get q_g ∝ h_g^{~5}. For m = 1 (linear viscous sliding) still q_g ∝ h_g^{~4.5}. The point is that flux is a strongly superlinear function of grounding-line thickness — small increases in h_g produce large increases in discharge.\n\nCombine that with hydrostatic flotation at the grounding line: h_g = (ρ_w/ρ_i) × |b_g|, where b_g is the bed depth below sea level (assumed negative). Thicker ice floats in deeper water. So *grounding-line thickness is determined by bed depth*, and flux is a strong function of bed depth.\n\n**Retrograde beds are unstable**\n\nConsider a grounding line at position x_g sitting on a bed that slopes down toward the ice-sheet interior (retrograde, ∂b/∂x < 0 in the retreat direction). Perturb the grounding line by δx inland. The new grounding-line bed is deeper, so h_g is larger, so q_g is larger by the strongly superlinear relation. Larger flux means more mass loss, thinning, and further retreat. The perturbation amplifies — the system is linearly unstable.\n\nOn a prograde bed (shallowing inland), the same perturbation decreases h_g, decreases q_g, and the perturbation is damped. Stable.\n\nSchoof\'s insight was to show that *steady states only exist on prograde beds*, and the jump across a bed high from prograde-to-retrograde is the trigger for MISI-type runaway retreat until the next prograde segment is reached.\n\nQualifications: the 2007 derivation is for 2D flow (flowline), no buttressing, steady state. Gudmundsson et al. (2012, The Cryosphere) showed that lateral buttressing from ice shelves pinned on bumps or side walls can stabilize grounding lines even on retrograde beds. The corollary is that *loss of buttressing* (ice-shelf thinning, pinning-point detachment) unpins a grounding line that was previously held on a retrograde bed, initiating retreat.\n\n**West Antarctic geometry**\n\nThe Amundsen Sea sector has textbook MISI geometry. The Thwaites grounding line sits near the top of a prograde-to-retrograde transition and the bed deepens to >2000 m below sea level going inland toward the WAIS interior. Pine Island Glacier\'s bed is similarly retrograde behind a former pinning ridge that the grounding line has already retreated off (circa 1970s based on re-analysis of old imagery). Both have been thinning and accelerating, with grounding-line retreat of 10-35 km since the 1990s depending on location, documented by InSAR (Rignot, Mouginot), CryoSat-2 altimetry, and Landsat feature tracking.\n\nJoughin, Smith, and Medley (2014, Science) argued the Thwaites retreat may already be committed — past a bed ridge and into retrograde geometry where MISI runs. Subsequent work (Milillo et al. 2019 Sci Advances on the Thwaites "ocean door," Davis et al. 2023) refined but hasn\'t overturned this. How fast the retreat proceeds is debated but the direction is not.\n\n**MICI — what\'s the claim and where it stands**\n\nMICI (marine ice cliff instability) is the hypothesis of DeConto & Pollard (2016, Nature): once floating ice shelves are lost (e.g., by hydrofracture driven by surface meltwater), the grounded ice margin presents a tall ice cliff. If the subaerial cliff height exceeds ~90-100 m, ice pressure near the cliff top induces tensile/shear failure and the cliff collapses. The freshly exposed face is again tall, and retreat runs away. DeConto & Pollard added MICI to their ice-sheet model and got 1+ m of Antarctic sea-level contribution by 2100 under RCP8.5, much higher than MISI-only estimates.\n\nThe case for MICI:\n- Bassis & Walker (2012) derived critical cliff heights for tensile failure around 90 m.\n- Jakobshavn and Helheim in Greenland show rapid marine-terminating retreats with tall calving faces.\n- Paleo sea-level (Last Interglacial, Pliocene) may require faster Antarctic retreat than MISI alone provides.\n\nThe case against (or at least for moderation):\n- Edwards et al. (2019, Nature) reanalyzed the DeConto-Pollard framework with a Bayesian calibration against paleo sea-level and showed MICI is not required to fit the observations.\n- Clerc et al. (2019) and Crawford et al. (2021, Nature Comms) showed that viscoplastic cliff failure requires faster thinning than assumed; cliffs up to ~135 m may be stable on human timescales, and even above threshold, collapse may be slower than the instantaneous failure DeConto-Pollard assumed.\n- No modern Antarctic ice cliffs exceed the critical height today; projection relies on getting shelves to collapse first.\n- The Jakobshavn analog is imperfect — Greenland\'s fjords are narrow and differently buttressed.\n\n**Where the field currently sits**\n\n- MISI: physically unambiguous, actively ongoing in the Amundsen Sea, likely committed for Thwaites on centennial-to-millennial timescales. How fast it proceeds depends on buttressing evolution and ocean forcing.\n- MICI: possible but not required by paleo data and the cliff-failure physics may be slower than the 2016 formulation assumed. It\'s a tail-risk mechanism — if shelves collapse broadly and cliffs taller than ~135 m form, MICI could run. Most recent process-model work has dialed back its contribution.\n\nCurrent IPCC AR6 "low-likelihood, high-impact" storyline essentially is: MISI is happening; MICI is a plausible amplifier that\'s not settled. For your own work, if you\'re citing papers that invoke MICI as established, you should know that\'s contested. MISI papers generally are on much firmer physical and observational ground.', 'leakage_group': 'conv_493', 'source_prompt_hash': '3cac1641de9d6056646cb61328e262dd0df6dc68756941be498d6f6c7fa13453', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 469, 'reference_tokens': 1532, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not repeat the thinking boundary or reopen thinking\nin the final answer. Do not quote these instructions.\n\nSubject: earth_science\nSubject-specific reasoning guidance:\nIdentify interacting Earth systems and the relevant spatial and temporal scales.\nConnect physical, chemical, biological, or geological processes to the observations being explained.\nCheck scale consistency, feedbacks, conservation where relevant, and plausible competing explanations."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Marine ice sheet instability gets thrown around a lot in Antarctic papers but I want the actual mechanics. Why is a reverse-sloped bed unstable, what's the flux-thickness relation, and is the MISI framework still holding up given the MICI debate?"}], 'completion': [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Thinking Process:\n\n1.  **Analyze the Request:**\n    *   **Topic:** Marine Ice Sheet Instability (MISI) in the context of Antarctic ice sheets.\n    *   **Specific Questions:**\n        1.  Why is a reverse-sloped bed unstable? (Mechanics).\n        2.  What\'s the flux-thickness relation? (The critical thickness condition).\n