{'conversation_id': 'conv_3019', 'example_id': 'conv_3019_message_4', 'category': 'chemistry', 'prompt_hash': '86e5bb1fd57651c94934211bc7ce7305235ff4245f751f2b9694ace1c7385da0', 'user_question': "Propionyl-(S)-4-benzyl-oxazolidinone on the acyl side. Aldehyde is (R)-2-methylbutanal — I'm making a polyketide fragment and this aldehyde comes from an earlier step, freshly distilled. I ran the benzaldehyde control and got 93:7. So it's the aldehyde.", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an experienced synthetic organic chemist. Discuss asymmetric synthesis with real mechanistic depth: Zimmerman-Traxler, chelation control, auxiliary removal. Admit when experimental reality differs from textbook rationalization.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I'm running an Evans aldol with the oxazolidinone auxiliary, dibutylboron triflate, and DIEA in DCM at -78 °C. I'm getting the 'Evans syn' product, which is what I want, but the dr is only 4:1. Everyone says these reactions should give >95:5 dr. What am I doing wrong?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "4:1 is a red flag — genuine Evans aldols with standard substrates should run 95:5 or better. Let me walk you through the diagnostic tree in rough order of likelihood.\n\n**1. Your Bu₂BOTf is probably dead or half-dead.** This is by far the most common cause of degraded Evans aldol selectivity in graduate labs. Dibutylboron triflate is exquisitely moisture-sensitive; it hydrolyzes slowly in the bottle even at -20 °C under argon. A fresh solution should be colorless to very faint yellow. If yours is orange, amber, or has a precipitate, it's dead. Three remedies:\n\n- Buy a freshly-manufactured ampoule (Aldrich lot codes matter — older stock fails more often than not).\n- Distill it yourself: Bu₃B + TfOH in DCM, distill at reduced pressure. This is standard Evans group prep.\n- Switch to Sn(OTf)₂ + Et₃N (Mukaiyama-Paterson variant) or TiCl₄ + DIEA (Crimmins/Ghosh variant — titanium enolates of the oxazolidinone) as a backup. Crimmins conditions are actually more tolerant of mediocre reagent quality.\n\n**2. Your DIEA isn't dry.** DIEA absorbs water readily. If it's been open on your bench for weeks, redistill from CaH₂. Alternatively, freshly opened bottles of sure-seal DIEA are usually fine.\n\n**3. Enolization protocol.** Standard: add Bu₂BOTf at -78 °C, stir 5 min, add DIEA dropwise, stir 30-45 min at -78 °C (longer for hindered acyl groups), then add aldehyde as a -78 °C DCM solution. Common errors: adding DIEA too fast (local overheating), not waiting long enough for enolization, or adding aldehyde before enolate is fully formed.\n\n**4. Aldehyde identity and purity.** What aldehyde are you using? Alpha-branched (iPr, cHex) can give slightly eroded dr — 90:10 is more typical there. Enolizable aldehydes undergo competing self-aldol. Purify aldehyde by distillation or filtration through basic alumina immediately before use; aldehydes oxidize to acids, which protonate your enolate.\n\n**5. Temperature excursions.** If your quench goes above -50 °C before the peroxide workup, retro-aldol/re-aldol can scramble stereochemistry. Quench cold with pH 7 phosphate buffer / MeOH, then add H₂O₂ (30%) dropwise at 0 °C to cleave the boronate.\n\n**Quick diagnostic.** Run the same reaction with benzaldehyde (unhindered, non-enolizable, cheap) and propionyl-(S)-4-benzyl-oxazolidinone. If *that* gives 4:1, your reagent is dead. If it gives 95:5, your original aldehyde or its workup is the problem.\n\nWhat's the specific acyl group on the oxazolidinone and what's the aldehyde? I can tighten the advice."}], 'reference_answer': "That's the problem right there. You're in a **matched/mismatched double-stereodifferentiation** situation, and with α-methyl aldehydes it's a well-known failure mode of Evans aldols. The aldehyde's own Felkin-Anh preference is fighting the auxiliary.\n\n**What's happening mechanistically.** In a standard Evans aldol with an achiral aldehyde, the Zimmerman-Traxler chair TS sets both new stereocenters: the α-stereocenter by the auxiliary, the β-stereocenter by the syn-selective chair. With (R)-2-methylbutanal, the aldehyde C-H-face choice is no longer free — Felkin-Anh considerations say the ethyl group wants to sit anti-periplanar to the incoming enolate. If the face Felkin prefers is the *same* face the Evans TS wants to attack, you're 'matched' and get >95:5. If they demand opposite faces, you're 'mismatched' and the two contributions partially cancel, giving 3:1 to 6:1. Your 4:1 is textbook mismatched.\n\nEvans himself documented this (*JACS* 1981, 1991) and it's a staple of Paterson's polyketide methodology reviews. α-Methyl aldehydes are the canonical case; α-alkoxy aldehydes (chelation-capable) are a different story.\n\n**Your options, in rough order of how much work they take.**\n\n**Option 1: Crimmins titanium enolate (recommended first try).** Swap the oxazolidinone for the corresponding *N*-propionyl (*S*)-4-benzyl-1,3-thiazolidine-2-thione and use TiCl₄ (1.05 equiv) / (-)-sparteine (1.1 equiv) / NMP (1 equiv) in DCM at -78 °C. Crimmins (*Org Lett* 2000, 2, 2165) showed this combination often rescues mismatched α-substituted aldehyde cases because the Ti enolate goes through a different (open or twist-boat) TS with different sensitivity to the aldehyde α-stereocenter. Many groups running polyketide syntheses (Paterson, Ghosh, Smith) switched to Crimmins chemistry specifically for cases like yours.\n\n**Option 2: Flip the auxiliary.** Switch to (*R*)-4-benzyl-oxazolidinone. This converts mismatched → matched and you should see dr jump to >95:5. The catch: you'll get the enantiomer of the aldol product relative to your original plan. You may need to reroute downstream stereochemistry, but for a polyketide fragment this often works out cleanly because you can also flip the aldehyde enantiomer or pair with the other antipode later.\n\n**Option 3: Paterson boron aldol with a chiral boron.** Use (-)-Ipc₂BOTf (diisopinocampheylboron triflate) with an achiral propionate ester instead of the auxiliary. The Ipc group's chirality dominates facial selectivity and can override the aldehyde's Felkin bias. Standard Paterson conditions: propionate, (-)-Ipc₂BOTf, Et₃N, -78 °C.\n\n**Option 4: Mukaiyama aldol with a chiral Lewis acid.** TMS ketene acetal of the propionate + (*R*)- or (*S*)-BINOL-TiCl₂ or Carreira-style catalyst. Different TS geometry, so different match/mismatch behavior.\n\n**What I'd actually do.** Start with Crimmins-Ti on your existing substrate — it's the smallest deviation from your current setup. If that gives dr <90:10, switch to Paterson (-)-Ipc₂BOTf on a simple propionate; the aldol product can be transesterified onto whatever acyl handle you need downstream.\n\n**One validation step.** Run Crimmins-Ti on benzaldehyde first to confirm your thiazolidinethione and TiCl₄ stocks are good, then graduate to (*R*)-2-methylbutanal. Don't debug two reagent changes at once.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_3019', 'source_prompt_hash': '4d8d0e6e74db5a9839368d74cb45cda459493203ac5017361437adf4ed4d84f3', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 1294, 'reference_tokens': 919, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal