 84%|████████▍ | 144/171 [7:03:00<1:24:05, 186.86s/it][RANK 0] All reward functions returned None for the following kwargs:
{'conversation_id': 'conv_4193', 'example_id': 'conv_4193_message_2', 'category': 'science', 'prompt_hash': 'f4d6a840eae3ee07d874eb6ff7a853d4913a9fd961f0cc8a74ee09eed92f0386', 'user_question': "Why do wounds swell and turn red when they're healing?", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a science and technology educator. Make complex topics accessible.'}], 'reference_answer': "The swelling and redness around a wound are not signs of something going wrong — they are the visible manifestations of your immune system's carefully orchestrated inflammatory response, a process so fundamental to survival that its four cardinal signs were described by the Roman physician Celsus nearly two thousand years ago: redness, swelling, heat, and pain.\n\nThe cascade begins within seconds of tissue damage. Broken cells spill their contents — ATP, DNA, potassium ions — into the surrounding tissue. These molecules, called damage-associated molecular patterns, serve as alarm signals. If bacteria have entered through the wound, their cell wall components (lipopolysaccharide, peptidoglycan) act as additional alarms called pathogen-associated molecular patterns. Both types of signal activate resident immune cells, particularly mast cells, which respond by degranulating — releasing preformed packets of histamine and other inflammatory mediators.\n\nHistamine acts immediately on nearby blood vessels. It causes arterioles to dilate, increasing blood flow to the damaged area. This is the source of the redness and warmth: more blood, flowing through wider vessels, makes the tissue visibly red and detectably warmer than surrounding skin. Histamine also causes the endothelial cells lining small blood vessels to contract slightly, opening gaps between them. Plasma — the protein-rich fluid component of blood — leaks through these gaps into the surrounding tissue, carrying with it antibodies, complement proteins, and clotting factors. The accumulation of this fluid in the tissue is what produces swelling, technically called edema.\n\nThe swelling serves multiple purposes. The leaked plasma proteins include complement, which can directly destroy bacteria by punching holes in their membranes, and antibodies, which tag pathogens for destruction. Clotting factors begin forming a fibrin mesh that walls off the damaged area and limits the spread of infection. The fluid pressure itself helps physically flush debris and microbes from the wound.\n\nMeanwhile, the dilated, leaky blood vessels set the stage for immune cell recruitment. Blood flow slows in the expanded vessels, allowing white blood cells — first neutrophils, then monocytes — to roll along the vessel wall, stick to adhesion molecules on the endothelium, and squeeze between endothelial cells to enter the tissue. This process, called diapedesis, delivers the cellular heavy hitters of the immune response to the exact site of damage. Neutrophils arrive within minutes and immediately begin engulfing bacteria and dead tissue. Monocytes follow within hours and differentiate into macrophages that clean up debris and coordinate the transition from inflammation to tissue repair.\n\nPain, the fourth cardinal sign, results from prostaglandins sensitizing nerve endings and bradykinin directly activating pain receptors. This is also purposeful: pain discourages you from using or exposing the injured area, giving it time to heal. The entire process — uncomfortable as it is — represents the body choosing short-term local disruption to prevent systemic catastrophe.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_4193', 'source_prompt_hash': '5c20eb4316396844fbd8846343ff85fcd01e1d7b803c4dbed4a902f11b92a5b6', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 423, 'reference_tokens': 593, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not repeat the thinking boundary or reopen thinking\nin the final answer. Do not quote these instructions.\n\nSubject: science\nSubject-specific reasoning guidance:\nChoose the scientific framework appropriate to the question and identify assumptions and uncertainty.\nBuild an evidence-supported explanation with valid logical or causal connections.\nTest consistency, plausible alternatives, and the support for any s