{'conversation_id': 'conv_7456', 'example_id': 'conv_7456_message_2', 'category': 'medicine', 'prompt_hash': 'fc7546feabca89db1e139a083bedbd748b489deb017f872ae802315686860625', 'user_question': 'Anticoagulation in cancer-associated VTE used to be LMWH, now DOACs are first-line. What changed?', 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are an attending hematologist explaining how a major treatment paradigm shifted. Engage with the evidence directly and address the user's frustration."}], 'reference_answer': 'The shift was driven by four solid trials between 2018 and 2020. The LMWH-dominant era came from CLOT (Lee et al., NEJM 2003), which compared dalteparin to warfarin in cancer patients and showed dalteparin halved recurrent VTE (9% vs 17%). That study was correct given the comparator — warfarin is bad in cancer because of nutritional swings, drug interactions with chemotherapy, and procedure interruptions. So LMWH became the standard, and that was the right answer for fifteen years.\n\nWhat changed is that DOACs were finally tested head-to-head against LMWH in cancer-VTE-specific trials, not just extrapolated from non-cancer VTE data.\n\nHokusai-VTE Cancer (Raskob et al., NEJM 2018, n=1050). Edoxaban 60 mg daily versus dalteparin for 6–12 months. Composite of recurrent VTE or major bleeding was 12.8% edoxaban versus 13.5% dalteparin — non-inferior. Recurrent VTE was lower with edoxaban (7.9% vs 11.3%); major bleeding was higher (6.9% vs 4.0%). The bleeding signal was concentrated in upper GI malignancies.\n\nSELECT-D (Young et al., JCO 2018, n=406). Rivaroxaban versus dalteparin. Recurrent VTE 4% versus 11% (favoring rivaroxaban); major bleeding 6% versus 4%. Clinically relevant non-major bleeding 13% versus 4%. Same pattern: better recurrence prevention, more bleeding, GI cancer–dominant.\n\nCaravaggio (Agnelli et al., NEJM 2020, n=1170). Apixaban 10 mg BID x 7 days then 5 mg BID versus dalteparin. Recurrent VTE 5.6% versus 7.9% (non-inferior, trend favoring apixaban). Major bleeding 3.8% versus 4.0% — equivalent. Critically, no excess GI bleeding in luminal GI cancer subgroup. This trial moved the needle decisively because it showed you could get DOAC convenience without the bleeding cost.\n\nADAM-VTE (McBane et al., 2020, n=300). Apixaban versus dalteparin. Major bleeding 0% versus 1.4%; VTE recurrence 3.4% versus 14.1% (favoring apixaban).\n\nThe consistent finding: DOACs are non-inferior or superior for recurrence prevention, with apixaban showing the cleanest safety profile and rivaroxaban/edoxaban showing excess GI bleeding in luminal GI cancers.\n\nCurrent guidelines (ASCO 2023, ITAC 2022, ASH 2021, NCCN) recommend:\n\n- Apixaban or rivaroxaban as first-line for most cancer-associated VTE.\n- Edoxaban as an alternative (requires LMWH lead-in for 5 days).\n- LMWH preferred in: luminal GI malignancy (especially gastric, gastroesophageal, and unresected colorectal), genitourinary tumors with mucosal lesions, severe renal impairment (CrCl under 30), drug interactions with strong P-gp/CYP3A4 modulators (some chemotherapy regimens, certain TKIs), and active mucositis or thrombocytopenia.\n- Avoid DOACs in primary brain tumors with uncertainty around hemorrhage risk — data are limited.\n- Duration: indefinite while cancer is active or on systemic therapy; reassess at 6 months and periodically thereafter.\n\nA few caveats. Patients on full-dose anticoagulation with platelets under 50 typically need dose reduction or LMWH (which is more titratable). Patients on cytotoxic chemotherapy with predictable thrombocytopenic nadirs may also benefit from LMWH agility. And catheter-associated thrombosis in cancer was underrepresented in these trials.\n\nSo the answer to your frustration: the standard changed because the evidence changed. CLOT was right when warfarin was the alternative; Caravaggio reset the comparison. Apixaban specifically is now the cleanest first-line choice for most patients.', 'leakage_group': 'conv_7456', 'source_prompt_hash': '39dc3c6a70648b25ccade4c7e4947c237166b78b02542b90c9e36c9b2efd8443', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 446, 'reference_tokens': 958, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not repeat the thinking boundary or reopen thinking\nin the final answer. Do not quote these instructions.\n\nSubject: medicine\nSubject-specific reasoning guidance:\nIdentify the relevant physiology, population, clinical context, and strength of available evidence.\nExplain mechanisms or interpret evidence with justified qualifications and appropriate uncertainty.\nCheck confounding, contrary evidence, applicability to the population, and unwarranted causal generalization."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Anticoagulation in cancer-associated VTE used to be LMWH, now DOACs are first-line. What changed?'}], 'completion': [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Thinking Process:\n\n1.  **Analyze the Request:**\n    *   Subject: Medicine (specifically oncology/hematology).\n    *   Question: "Anticoagulation in cancer-associated VTE used to be LMWH, now DOACs are first-line. What changed?"\n    *   Constraint Checklist & Confidence Score:\n        1. Answer scientifically accurately? Yes.\n        2. Clear and at depth requested? Yes.\n        3. Use user\'s actual question and relevant context? Yes.\n        4. Identify/correct false premises? Need to check if the premise is entirely accurate.\n        5. Distinguish facts/assumptions/conclusions/uncertainty? Yes.\n        6. No inventing sources/mechanisms/experience? Yes.\n        7. Concise correct account? Yes.\n        8. Native thinking trace? Yes.\n        9. Final answer self-contained? Yes.\n        10. Check confounding/contrary evidence/applicability? Yes.\n    *   Formatting: Native thinking (invisible) -> `'}]}
