he color outcome isn\'t as starkly dramatic as "totally different" if interpreted as a completely different universe of hues. The fundamental difference is the scattering regime.\n\nEarth’s sky is dominated by **Rayleigh scattering**, which occurs off individual gas molecules (nitrogen and oxygen). Because these molecules are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light, the scattering efficiency scales dramatically with wavelength ($\\propto 1/\\lambda^4$), suppressing longer wavelengths and amplifying the shorter ones.\n\nMars’ sky, by contrast, is dominated by **Mie scattering** from ubiquitous iron-oxide dust suspended in its thin atmosphere. Dust particles are roughly the size of or larger than visible wavelengths. Consequently, Mie scattering becomes largely independent of wavelength (or has much weaker dependence than Rayleigh). It scatters all colors relatively evenly, producing a white or grey haze rather than a selective blue.\n\nSo the physical mechanism you’re tracking is right: it’s the change from molecular scattering to particulate scattering that flips the color palette.\n\nHowever, the *result* is nuanced:\n- **Clear skies**: When the dust layer is thin, the remaining air molecules still provide some Rayleigh scattering. The sky appears a pale, washed-out blue or light violet, rather than deep blue.\n- **Hazy/Stormy skies**: When the atmosphere fills with dust (even submicron amounts), the Mie scattering washes out the selective color filtering entirely. The sky takes on a milky white, yellowish, or salmon tint, depending on the dust density. During a major global dust storm, the entire hemisphere can appear uniformly red or orange as the dust suspends enough to scatter red light significantly along longer path lengths.\n\nTherefore, it is correct that the dust is the primary differentiator for the sky’s color, but the physics remains universal—the difference lies in the composition (dust vs. gas) rather than the planet itself having unique rul