torname{arcsinh}(1) = \\ln(1+\\sqrt{2})$. So $K_c = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln(1+\\sqrt{2})$.\n    *   Let\'s check. Onsager (1944): $\\sinh(2J/k_B T_c) = 1$.\n    *   Calculation: $T_c^{\\text{exact}} = \\frac{2J}{k_B \\operatorname{arcsinh}(1)} = \\frac{2J}{k_B \\ln(1+\\sqrt{2})}$.\n    *   Numerical value: $\\ln(1+\\sqrt{2}) \\approx 0.8814$. So $T_c^{\\text{exact}} \\approx \\frac{2}{0.8814} \\frac{J}{k_B} \\approx 2.269 \\frac{J}{k_B}$.\n    *   Compare to MFT: $T_c^{\\text{MFT}} = 4J/k_B$.\n    *   Ratio: $2.27 / 4 \\approx 0.57$. MFT overestimates significantly.\n\n4.  **Step 3: Why MFT is Wrong.**\n    *   MFT assumes independence of fluctuations between sites (factorizes distribution). It replaces discrete correlations $\\langle s_i s_j \\rangle$ with products of averages $\\langle s_i \\rangle \\langle s_j \\rangle$.\n    *   In 2D, long-range correlations are crucial near $T_c$. Fluctuations are large.\n    *   Mermin-Wagner theorem isn\'t relevant here because the order parameter is scalar (Ising) and 2D allows ordering (unlike XY model).\n    *   The main flaw: MFT neglects fluctuations (specifically spatial correlations of order parameters) which contribute significantly to thermodynamic properties near the critical point.\n    *   Dimensionality dependence: In high dimensions (d > 4), MFT becomes exact (Upper critical dimension). In d=2, corrections are significant.\n    *   Specific explanation: MFT treats neighbors independently; they actually correlate strongly in the 2D case near phase transition due to lack of screening/diffusion mechanisms. It ignores topology of the lattice more than just connectivity.\n    *   Key phrase: "Fluctuation effects."\n\n5.  **Step 4: Structure the Response.**\n    *   Derivation section: Hamiltonian, effective field, partition function/free energy, self-consistency condition, finding $T_c$.\n    *   Comparison section: Quote exact value ($T_c \\approx 2.27 J/k_B$ vs $4 J/k_B$).\n    *   Critique section: Why MFT fails (fluctu