hink>\n\nTo find the critical temperature ($T_c$) of the 2D Ising model using mean-field theory (MFT), we start with the Hamiltonian and the fundamental assumption of the theory.\n\n**1. Derivation via Mean-Field Theory**\nThe Hamiltonian for a ferromagnetic Ising model on a lattice is:\n$$ H = -J \\sum_{\\langle i,j \\rangle} s_i s_j $$\nwhere $s_i = \\pm 1$, $J > 0$ is the exchange interaction, and $\\langle i,j \\rangle$ denotes nearest-neighbor pairs. On a square 2D lattice, the coordination number is $z = 4$.\n\nMean-field theory approximates the interaction between spins by replacing the fluctuating local environment of a spin $s_i$ with a static average magnetization $m = \\langle s \\rangle$. The total field acting on spin $i$ is treated as an effective field:\n$$ h_{\\text{eff}} = J z m $$\nThe contribution to the partition function or free energy depends on the probability of finding spin $s_i$ in this field. The condition for equilibrium (or minimizing the free energy) leads to the self-consistency equation:\n$$ m = \\tanh\\left(\\frac{J z m}{k_B T}\\right) $$\nHere, $m=0$ is the paramagnetic solution for $T > T_c$. The transition to a spontaneously magnetized phase ($m \\neq 0$) occurs when the slope of the curve $y = \\tanh(J z m / k_B T)$ at $m=0$ exceeds 1.\nExpanding $\\tanh(x) \\approx x$ for small arguments:\n$$ m \\approx \\frac{J z m}{k_B T} $$\nNon-trivial solutions exist when:\n$$ \\frac{J z}{k_B T_c} = 1 \\implies k_B T_c^{\\text{MFT}} = J z $$\nFor a square 2D lattice ($z=4$):\n$$ T_c^{\\text{MFT}} = \\frac{4J}{k_B} $$\n\n**2. Honest Assessment: How Wrong is the Answer?**\nThe calculated value $T_c^{\\text{MFT}} = 4J/k_B$ is significantly incorrect for the actual physical system described by the exact solution.\n\n**Comparison with Exact Solution:**\nLandau (Onsager) solved the 2D Ising model exactly in 1944. The critical temperature is determined by:\n$$ \\sinh\\left(\\frac{2J}{k_B T_c}\\right) = 1 $$\nThis yields the numerical value:\n$$ \\frac{2J}{k_B T_c} = \\ln(1 + \\sqrt{2}) \\approx 0.8814 $$\n$$ T_c^{\\text{Exact}} = \\frac{2J}{0.8814 k_B} \\approx 2.269 \\frac{J}{k_B} $$\n\n**Error Quantification:**\n$$ \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{T_c^{\\text{MFT}}}{T_c^{\\text{Exact}}} \\approx \\frac{4.0}{2.27} \\approx 1.76 $$\nThe Mean-Field Theory overestimates the critical temperature by about **76%**. It predicts a transition that does not occur at the true physical parameters.\n\n**Why is it Wrong?**\n1.  **Neglect of Fluctuations:** MFT assumes that the average field experienced by each spin is the sum of all neighbors\' values without accounting for their statistical correlation ($\\langle s_i s_j \\rangle \\neq \\langle s_i \\rangle \\langle s_j \\rangle$). Near $T_c$, spatial fluctuations become macroscopic (diverging correlation length $\\xi$). MFT predicts a correlation length that grows only as $(T_c - T)^{-1/2}$ (roughly), whereas in reality, the physics is dominated by collective behavior that lowers the stability of the ordered phase relative to MFT.\n2.  **Dimensional Crossover:** MFT corresponds to the limit of infinite dimensions or higher-order expansions that ignore short-range correlations. It is known as a "high-dimensional" theory (exact in $d \\to \\infty$, valid as $d \\to 4^+$). The 2D case lies far below the upper critical dimension ($d_c = 4$). Below $d=4$, fluctuations are strong enough to invalidate the assumption of independent sites.\n3.  **Entropy/Mean Interaction Trade-off:** By treating neighbors as fixed, MFT artificially suppresses the entropic cost of ordering compared to the constrained exact dynamics, pushing the transition to lower temperatures (Wait, higher T, higher disorder needed to melt). It essentially claims spins stay aligned longer than they actually do because it underestimates the entropy reduction caused by forcing alignment.\n\n**Summary of Validity:**\nWhile useful for a rough qualitative understanding of second-order phase transitions and calculating exponents in $d > 4$, MFT provides quantitatively inaccurate predictions for the 2D Ising model. It fails primarily because it neglects the cooperative nature of critical fluctuations which define the physics of the phase transition in low dimensions.\n\n</think>\n\n### Final Answer\n\n**Derivation of Critical Temperature via Mean-Field Theory (MFT):**\nWe begin with the Ising Hamiltonian on a lattice with coordination number $z$:\n$$ H = -J \\sum_{\\langle i,j \\rangle} s_i s_j $$\nMFT replaces the interaction term with an effective molecular field $h_{\\text{eff}} = J z m$, where $m = \\langle s \\rangle$ is the average magnetization. Equilibrium requires minimizing the free energy, leading to the self-consistency condition:\n$$ m = \\tanh\\left( \\frac{J z m}{k_B T} \\right) $$\nAt the critical temperature $T_c$, the non-zero solution for $m$ appears. This occurs when the linear expansion $\\tanh(x) \\approx x$ matches the slope of the RHS relative to the LHS $m$:\n$$ \\frac{J z}{k_B T_c} = 1 \\implies k_B T_c^{\\text{MFT}} = J z $$\nFor a 2D square lattice, $z=4$, yielding:\n$$ T_c^{\\text{MFT}} = \\frac{4J}{k_B} $$\n\n**Evaluation of Accuracy:**\nThis result is **incorrect** for the exact physical 2D Ising system.\n1.  **Exact Critical Temperature:** Landau-Onsager derived the exact solution using transfer matrices, giving the relation $\\sinh(2J/k_B T_c) = 1$. This evaluates to $T_c^{\\text{Exact}} \\approx 2.269 \\, J/k_B$.\n2.  **Magnitude of Error:** MFT overestimates $T_c$ by a factor of $\\approx 1.76$ (or roughly 76%). The theory predicts the ordered phase is much more stable than it physically is.\n3.  **Physical Reason:** MFT assumes spins behave independently within an average field. In reality, close to $T_c$ in 2D, strong long-range fluctuations and correlations dominate ($\\xi \\to \\infty$). These collective fluctuations enhance the susceptibility and destabilize the magnetic order earlier than the uncoupled-site approximation predicts. MFT is qualitatively valid in $d \\geq 4$ (where fluctuations are less significant relative to the mean field) but quantitatively fails in $d=2$ where the critical exponents deviate significantly from mean-field values.'}]}
