{'conversation_id': 'conv_7561', 'example_id': 'conv_7561_message_4', 'category': 'science', 'prompt_hash': '47d217dad1ae45a63db5641dfe9c0626f1857b77ee03c9365edb96b2e110499c', 'user_question': "He's pushing back: 'Sequential analysis lets you peek at data and stop early. Why can't I just do that?' Is he right that sequential designs solve this?", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are advising researchers on study design and pre-registration. Use concrete examples, name specific failure modes, and assume technical literacy.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "My grad student keeps asking why we have to pre-register everything if we already know what we're testing. He says it just slows things down. I want to give him a concrete example showing what pre-registration actually prevents — ideally with a real garden-of-forking-paths scenario. Help me explain it."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "The key insight your student is missing: 'I know what I'm testing' is not the same as 'all my analytic decisions are locked.' The damage from researcher degrees of freedom happens in micro-decisions made after seeing data, not in the high-level hypothesis. Here's a worked example.\n\n**Setup**\n\nYou run a study testing whether a 4-week mindfulness app reduces workplace stress. N=200, randomized to app or waitlist control. You have a clear hypothesis: app < control on stress at week 4. Sounds locked down, right?\n\n**The forks you didn't think were forks**\n\n1. **Outcome measure.** You collected the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS-10), the DASS-21 stress subscale, and self-rated 'overall stress 1-10.' Three plausible primary outcomes. After seeing data, you pick the one that hits p<.05.\n2. **Time point.** You measured at weeks 2, 4, and 8. The pre-registration said 'week 4,' but you didn't pre-register that — and week 8 looks better, so 'long-term effects' becomes the framing.\n3. **Exclusion criteria.** You decide post-hoc to exclude people who used the app fewer than 3 times ('didn't really get the intervention'). This is a per-protocol analysis dressed as primary, and it always favors the treatment because non-compliers are usually disengaged people with worse baseline outcomes.\n4. **Outliers.** Someone in the control group reported a death in the family and stress spiked to 9/10. Drop them? Winsorize? Two defensible choices, two different p-values.\n5. **Covariates.** You can adjust for baseline stress, age, gender, job role. Each combination is a different model. With 4 covariates, you have 16 combinations.\n6. **Subgroups.** The main effect is null but it 'works' for women, or for people under 35, or for those with high baseline stress. You report the subgroup as if it were the planned analysis.\n7. **Transformation.** Stress scores are skewed. Log? Square root? Raw? Each gives a slightly different result.\n8. **Sample size.** You planned N=200 but data collection is slow. After N=150, results are 'trending' (p=.08). You collect 50 more and stop when p<.05 ('optional stopping').\n\n**The math**\n\nSimmons, Nelson, and Simonsohn's 2011 simulation in *Psychological Science* — 'False-Positive Psychology' — showed that combining just FOUR of these degrees of freedom (two DVs, optional stopping, one covariate, one subgroup split) inflates the false positive rate from the nominal 5% to roughly 61% when the null is true. Gelman and Loken's 2013 'Garden of Forking Paths' argument is even subtler: you don't need to consciously p-hack. You just need to make different decisions if the data had looked different, and the unconditional false positive rate is already inflated.\n\n**What pre-registration actually locks**\n\nA proper pre-reg specifies, before you see data:\n\n- The single primary DV (others are secondary, labeled).\n- The single primary time point.\n- Exact exclusion criteria, with thresholds.\n- Outlier-handling rule (e.g., 'Winsorize at 3 SD').\n- Statistical model with covariates listed.\n- Sample size and stopping rule (or sequential analysis plan).\n- Which subgroups are confirmatory vs exploratory.\n- Direction of predicted effect.\n\nNot pre-registered = exploratory. You can still run any analysis you want, you just have to label it.\n\n**The student's real concern, addressed**\n\nPre-registration doesn't ban exploration. It bans pretending exploratory analyses were confirmatory. If your week-8 women-under-35 result is interesting, write it up as a hypothesis-generating finding to be tested in study 2. That's how science is supposed to work. The Many Labs and Registered Reports literatures (Scheel et al. 2021 found ~96% of RR results land where the methods predict, vs ~96% of standard psych papers being 'positive' due to file-drawer + forking paths) show this pays off in replication rates.\n\nThe real time saving from pre-reg is on the back end: reviewers don't make you justify every analytic choice, and you don't have to spend three months in revision defending why you chose this DV over that one. The front-end cost is maybe a day of careful thinking. Tell your student to try it once on a real study and compare the review experience."}], 'reference_answer': "He's half-right, and the half he's missing is the load-bearing part.\n\n**Where he's right**\n\nGroup sequential designs and sequential probability ratio tests (SPRT) are legitimate frameworks that explicitly allow you to peek at data and stop early. They're standard in clinical trials. Every interim analysis in a Phase III pharma trial is technically optional stopping done right.\n\n**Where he's wrong**\n\nThese methods control Type I error only because they pre-specify, before data collection:\n\n1. The exact number of looks (e.g., 'we will look after 25%, 50%, 75%, 100% of N').\n2. The alpha-spending function (O'Brien-Fleming spends most alpha at the final look — early stopping requires very strong evidence; Pocock spends alpha equally; Lan-DeMets is flexible but the function shape is fixed in advance).\n3. The stopping rules — both efficacy and futility boundaries.\n4. How alpha is recovered if you don't stop early.\n\nThe critical values at each look are **inflated** to keep the family-wise Type I error at 5%. For example, with O'Brien-Fleming and 4 equally spaced looks, you need roughly p<.005 at the first look, p<.014 at the second, p<.022 at the third, and p<.043 at the fourth — not p<.05 at any of them. If you ignore this and use p<.05 at every look with 4 looks, your actual false positive rate is around 14%, not 5%.\n\nNaive optional stopping — 'I'll keep collecting until p<.05 then stop' — has a Type I error rate that approaches 100% as max N grows (this is the law of the iterated logarithm). Armitage, McPherson, and Rowe showed this in 1969; it's been re-derived many times since. Simmons et al. 2011 demonstrated it in simulation for psychology contexts.\n\n**The practical answer for your student**\n\nIf he wants to peek, here's the workflow:\n\n1. Decide max N and number of interim looks before collecting data.\n2. Choose an alpha-spending function (O'Brien-Fleming is conservative and popular; gsDesign in R or `ldbounds` will compute the boundaries).\n3. Pre-register the full design including stopping rules.\n4. Run the analysis at each pre-specified look using the adjusted critical value.\n5. Stop when a boundary is crossed, or run to max N if not.\n\nLakens (European Journal of Social Psychology, 2014) wrote a clean tutorial — 'Performing high-powered studies efficiently with sequential analyses' — specifically aimed at psychologists. Worth assigning him.\n\n**Bayesian alternative**\n\nIf he's philosophically allergic to frequentist corrections, Bayes factors (Rouder, Speckman, Sun, Morey, & Iverson, 2009) let you monitor evidence as data accumulates without stopping-rule corrections in the same form — though there's debate (Rouder 2014 vs de Heide & Grünwald 2017) about whether this is fully true. Practically, BFs have their own issues: prior sensitivity, harder to communicate to reviewers in some fields. But for someone who finds sequential designs ugly, it's worth knowing about.\n\n**The meta-point**\n\nAll three approaches — fixed-N, group sequential, and Bayesian — are legitimate. None of them allow what your student wants, which is to make stopping decisions ad hoc based on what the data look like. The whole point of inference is that the procedure has to be specifiable independent of the data; otherwise the math doesn't work. He's not being asked to give up flexibility, he's being asked to specify his flexibility in advance.", 'leakage_group': 'conv_7561', 'source_prompt_hash': '61a0893cbc694506f111df695d98470b1818f81fc7946896698bc013576387ac', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 1537, 'reference_tokens': 829, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not repeat the thinking boundary or reopen thinking\nin the final answer. Do not quote these instructions.\n\nSubject: science\nSubject-specific reasoning guidance:\nChoose the scientific framework appropriate to the question and identify assumptions and uncertainty.\nBuild an evidence-supported explanation with valid logical or causal connections.\nTest consistency, plausible alternatives, and the support for any specific or quantitative claims."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "My grad student keeps asking why we have to pre-register everything if we already know what we're testing. He says it just slows things down. I want to give him a concrete example showing what pre-registration actually prevents — ideally with a real garden-of-forking-paths scenario. Help me explain it."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "The key insight your student is missing: 'I know what I'm testing' is not the same as 'all my analytic decisions are locked.' The damage from researcher degrees of freedom happens in micro-decisions made after seeing data, not in the high-level hypothesis. Here's a worked example.\n\n**Setup**\n\nYou run a study testing whether a 4-week mindfulness app reduces workplace stress. N=200, randomized to app or waitlist control. You have a clear hypothesis: app < control on stress at week 4. Sounds locked down, right?\n\n**The forks you didn't think were forks**\n\n1. **Outcome measure.** You collected the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS-10), the DASS-21 stress subscale, and self-rated 'overall stress 1-10.' Three plausible primary outcomes. After seeing data, you pick the one that hits p<.05.\n2. **Time point.** You measured at weeks 2, 4, and 8. The pre-registration said 'week 4,' but you didn't pre-register that — and week 8 looks better, so 'long-term effects' becomes the framing.\n3. **Exclusion criteria.** You decide post-hoc to exclude people who used the app fewer than 3 times ('didn't really get the intervention'). This is a per-protocol analysis dressed as primary, and it always favors the treatment because non-compliers are usually disengaged people with worse baseline outcomes.\n4. **Outliers.** Someone in the control group reported a death in the family and stress spiked to 9/10. Drop them? Winsorize? Two defensible choices, two different p-values.\n5. **Covariates.** You can adjust for baseline stress, age, gender, job role. Each combination is a different model. With 4 covariates, you have 16 combinations.\n6. **Subgroups.** The main effect is null but it 'works' for women, or for people under 35, or for those with high baseline stress. You report the subgroup as if it were the planned analysis.\n7. **Transformation.** Stress scores are skewed. Log? Square root? Raw? Each gives a slightly different result.\n8. **Sample size.** You planned N=200 but data collection is slow. After N=150, results are 'trending' (p=.08). You collect 50 more and stop when p<.05 ('optional stopping').\n\n**The math**\n\nSimmons, Nelson, and Simonsohn's 2011 simulation in *Psychological Science* — 'False-Positive Psychology' — showed that combining just FOUR of these degrees of freedom (two DVs, optional stopping, one covariate, one subgroup split) inflates the false positive rate from the nominal 5% to roughly 61% when the null is true. Gelman and Loken's 2013 'Garden of Forking Paths' argument is even subtler: you don't need to consciously