{'conversation_id': 'conv_1961', 'example_id': 'conv_1961_message_2', 'category': 'biology', 'prompt_hash': '85d6c292d37e4dc405ae5abc091a193d0ea9e8fcc4a5a941ccdb3eca36edc4e0', 'user_question': "I'm an analyst running single-cell RNA-seq — UMAP embeddings keep being misinterpreted as continuous developmental trajectories. What's actually safe to conclude from a UMAP?", 'conversation_context': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are a computational biology mentor speaking to a working analyst. Be technical, name specific papers and pitfalls, and don't oversimplify."}], 'reference_answer': 'You are right to be frustrated. UMAP is the most over-interpreted plot in modern biology, and the misuses you\'re seeing are exactly the ones the methods literature has flagged repeatedly.\n\n**What UMAP actually does.** UMAP (McInnes, Healy, Melville 2018) constructs a fuzzy simplicial set from the high-dimensional data — essentially a weighted k-nearest-neighbor graph with edge weights derived from local density estimates — and then optimizes a low-dimensional embedding to approximate that graph under cross-entropy loss. The objective preserves *which points are near which other points* locally. It does not preserve distances, density, or global geometry, and it never claimed to.\n\n**What is safe to conclude:**\n\n1. **Cluster identity.** If a group of cells lands together and they share marker gene expression, that\'s a real signal — but the cluster boundary should be defined by the clustering algorithm (Leiden on the kNN graph), not by visual gaps in the UMAP. The visual gap is a layout artifact.\n\n2. **Local neighbor relationships among biologically similar cells.** Two cells that are immediate UMAP neighbors are usually transcriptomically similar. The converse is far weaker.\n\n3. **Qualitative differential analysis.** "Cluster 5 expresses CD8A and GZMB and clusters with other T cells" is a fine UMAP-supported claim. The UMAP is being used as a visualization of the kNN graph, which is the actual evidence.\n\n**What is NOT safe to conclude — and this is your problem:**\n\n1. **Continuous trajectories from UMAP shape.** A curving stripe of cells in UMAP space is not evidence of a developmental continuum. UMAP optimization frequently produces such shapes from discrete clusters or even from noise — Chari & Pachter (2023, *PLoS Computational Biology*, "The specious art of single-cell genomics") demonstrated UMAP producing biologically suggestive but spurious topologies on permuted data and even on Mammoth point clouds. The Mammoth example is a viral teaching case: the same algorithm with different seeds produces structurally different trajectories from the same data.\n\n2. **Distance between clusters.** If cluster A and cluster B sit far apart in UMAP and clusters A and C sit close, that does *not* mean A is more similar to C than to B. The optimization has no incentive to preserve that.\n\n3. **Density.** UMAP applies a local density normalization. A dense blob in the embedding does not mean a dense region in transcriptomic space; it can be the opposite.\n\n4. **Direction along an axis.** "Pseudotime increases left-to-right" read off a UMAP is meaningless unless an actual pseudotime tool computed it on the underlying graph — and even then, mapping it to a UMAP axis is just for display.\n\n**What to do instead for trajectories.**\n\n- **PAGA** (Wolf et al. 2019, *Genome Biology*) computes a coarse-grained graph between clusters with statistically meaningful connectivity. It\'s the soundest "do clusters connect?" tool.\n- **Slingshot** (Street et al. 2018) and **Monocle3** (Cao et al. 2019) infer pseudotime on the high-dimensional kNN graph. The UMAP is only the canvas they paint on.\n- **Diffusion maps and diffusion pseudotime** (Haghverdi et al. 2016) are explicitly designed to preserve trajectory geometry, unlike UMAP.\n- **RNA velocity** (La Manno et al. 2018; Bergen et al. *Nat Biotech* 2020 dynamical model) infers direction from spliced/unspliced ratios. But read Gorin, Fang, Chari, Pachter 2022 (*Mol Sys Bio*) before trusting velocity vectors — the steady-state assumptions break in many real datasets, especially when transcription rates change.\n\n**Practical defenses for your reports:**\n\n- Always plot the same data with at least two random seeds. If the trajectory disappears or rearranges, it was layout, not biology.\n- Report cluster connectivity from PAGA alongside the UMAP.\n- Run pseudotime on the graph, then color the UMAP by pseudotime — never the reverse.\n- When a collaborator gestures at a UMAP shape, ask them which marker genes vary along the supposed axis. If they can\'t say, the shape is hypothesis-free.\n- Consider supplementing with a force-directed layout (FA2, Fruchterman-Reingold on the kNN graph) which has different artifacts and helps triangulate.\n\nThe one-line summary you can use: UMAP is a visualization of a graph, not a metric space. Conclusions belong to the graph and the genes, not to the picture.', 'leakage_group': 'conv_1961', 'source_prompt_hash': '8171aad46fb8ddfcafc2f128b5d467c275f40da4dbe1bbdb0fcba7700107e285', 'scaffold_version': 'science-gspo-g-native-thinking-v1', 'prompt_tokens': 451, 'reference_tokens': 1016, 'epoch_padding': False, 'prompt': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You answer scientific questions accurately, clearly, and at the depth requested.\nUse the user's actual question and relevant context. Earlier assistant statements\nand the user's proposed explanations can be mistaken: identify and correct false\npremises rather than building on them. Distinguish established facts, assumptions,\nconditional conclusions, and uncertainty. Do not invent sources, measurements,\npersonal experience, or mechanisms. Prefer a concise correct account to unsupported\ndetail. Consider valid alternative explanations when they matter to the question.\n\nIn your native thinking, choose an appropriate framework and the assumptions that\nmatter, apply them with justified mathematical, logical, causal, or evidential\nreasoning, and check the result or explanation in a way useful for the question.\nA check should test a claim, inference, assumption, or limitation rather than\nmerely assert confidence. Correct errors that you discover. These are reasoning\nactivities, not separate labeled sections: use a natural thinking trace and do\nnot add special XML-style process or answer tags. You may interleave and revisit\nthese activities when useful; no fixed sequence of headings is required.\nKeep simple questions brief; do not manufacture calculations or a long derivation\nfor factual or conceptual questions. Concision can be fully adequate.\n\nThe chat template may already open the native thinking channel; do not open it\nagain in that case. Finish thinking with exactly one native </think> boundary.\nThen give a self-contained final answer in plain prose or mathematical notation,\nwith material uncertainty and qualifications as appropriate. The final answer\nmust make sense by itself. Do not repeat the thinking boundary or reopen thinking\nin the final answer. Do not quote these instructions.\n\nSubject: biology\nSubject-specific reasoning guidance:\nIdentify the relevant biological scale, entities, supported mechanisms, and conditions.\nConnect processes causally without inventing missing links or treating one pathway as universal.\nConsider exceptions, alternative mechanisms, evidence strength, and whether the claimed scale is justified."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I'm an analyst running single-cell RNA-seq — UMAP embeddings keep being misinterpreted as continuous developmental trajectories. What's actually safe to conclude from a UMAP?"}], 'completion': [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Thinking Process:\n\n1.  **Analyze the Request:**\n    *   **User:** Analyst running single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq).\n    *   **Problem:** UMAP embeddings are being misinterpreted as continuous developmental trajectories.\n    *   **Question:** What is actually safe to conclude from a UMAP?\n    *   **Subject:** Biology / Computational Biology (specifically scRNA-seq analysis).\n    *   **Constraints:** Accurate, clear, depth requested, identify false premises, distinguish facts/assumptions/uncertainty, no inventing sources/mechanisms, concise correct account, natural thinking trace ending in `'}]}
