nits, charge, applicable conditions, and mechanistic or thermodynamic consistency."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Compare GC vs LC — same family of separation but the chemistry of choice differs by analyte. What governs the choice and where do they overlap?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "GC and LC are first cousins — both are partition chromatography, both separate by repeated equilibration between a mobile and stationary phase along a column. The divergence is the phase-state of the mobile phase, and that single fact cascades into everything else.\n\n**The structural difference.**\n\nIn GC the mobile phase is an inert carrier gas (helium, hydrogen, nitrogen). It doesn't interact chemically with the analyte — it's a pure transport medium. All selectivity therefore comes from two sources: (1) analyte volatility (vapor pressure as a function of temperature), and (2) analyte interaction with the stationary phase (a thin film of polysiloxane or polyethylene glycol in a fused-silica capillary). You tune retention by changing temperature, and selectivity by changing column chemistry.\n\nIn LC the mobile phase is a liquid that *does* interact with the analyte — it dissolves it, competes with the stationary phase for it, and contributes its own polarity, hydrogen-bonding, and ionic character. Selectivity is two-handed: stationary phase chemistry *and* mobile phase composition. You tune retention by changing solvent strength (gradient elution), and selectivity by changing modifier or stationary phase.\n\nThis difference in the role of the mobile phase is why LC has more flexibility per column, while GC has more efficiency per unit length.\n\n**Analyte property windows.**\n\nGC requires the analyte to be:\n- **Volatile** at column temperatures, conventionally up to ~350-400 °C in high-temperature GC. Practical MW ceiling: ~600-800 Da for unfunctionalized hydrocarbons, lower for polar compounds whose vapor pressure is suppressed by H-bonding. Triglycerides (MW ~900) on 